{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"node_repl","tool":"js","arguments":{"code":"var fs = await import('node:fs/promises'); var root = '/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-j6g'; var paths = ['AGENTS.md', 'docs/design/datom.world.md', 'BRIEF-j6g.md', 'src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj']; for (var p of paths) { var s = await fs.readFile(root + '/' + p, 'utf8'); nodeRepl.write(p + '\\n' + s.split('\\n').map((l,i) => `${i+1}: ${l}`).join('\\n')); }","title":"Read architecture, brief, and workload harness"},"result":null,"error":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"node_repl","tool":"js","arguments":{"code":"var fs = await import('node:fs/promises'); var root = '/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-j6g'; var paths = ['AGENTS.md', 'docs/design/datom.world.md', 'BRIEF-j6g.md', 'src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj']; for (var p of paths) { var s = await fs.readFile(root + '/' + p, 'utf8'); nodeRepl.write(p + '\\n' + s.split('\\n').map((l,i) => `${i+1}: ${l}`).join('\\n')); }","title":"Read architecture, brief, and workload harness"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"ENOENT: no such file or directory, open '/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-j6g/BRIEF-j6g.md'"},{"type":"text","text":"AGENTS.md\n1: # ==============================================================================\n2: # datom.world – Development Rules & Architectural Foundations\n3: # ==============================================================================\n4: \n5: See [`docs/design/datom.world.md`](./docs/design/datom.world.md) for master architecture, core philosophy, foundational axioms, non-negotiable invariants, code style, development workflow, and operational guides (testing, team roles, agent delegation, website formats).\n6: docs/design/datom.world.md\n1: ---\n2: description: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n3: ---\n4: \n5: # DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n6: \n7: ## Core Philosophy\n8: \n9: Everything is data.\n10: Interpretation > abstraction.\n11: Explicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n12: Restrictions are a feature.\n13: \n14: ### Foundational Axioms\n15: \n16: 1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n17: 2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n18:    At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n19:    pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n20:    interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n21: 3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n22: 4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n23: \n24: ## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n25: \n26: - Do not introduce hidden global state.\n27: - Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n28: - Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n29: - Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n30: - Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n31: - Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n32: - Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n33: \n34: ## Tuples and Datoms\n35: \n36: Tuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n37: A datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n38: Source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n39: \n40: See [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n41: \n42: ## Streams\n43: \n44: `dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n45: Hosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\n46: Portable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\n47: Streams are values that can be sent through streams.\n48: Streams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n49: \n50: A stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n51: of interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\n52: semantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\n53: stream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\n54: the very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\n55: answers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\n56: exists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\n57: the other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\n58: and the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\n59: becomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\n60: the stream's own.\n61: \n62: Functions can take anything and return anything.\n63: Agents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n64: \n65: See [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\n66: See [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n67: \n68: ### Host Boundaries\n69: \n70: A host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n71: [datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n72: \n73: ## Architectural Commitments\n74: \n75: - **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n76: - **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n77: - **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n78: - **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n79: \n80: ## Design Principles\n81: \n82: Code quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\n83: Stigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n84: \n85: **Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\n86: composition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\n87: without collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n88: \n89: - `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n90:   ordinary streams.\n91: - ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n92:   interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n93: - The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n94: - The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n95:   auto-index observer.\n96: - Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n97:   interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n98: \n99: **Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\n100: canonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\n101: shape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n102: `dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\n103: query or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\n104: consumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\n105: retired `:global` tag in\n106: [`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\n107: case study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n108: \n109: See [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\n110: See [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n111: \n112: ## Code Style\n113: \n114: - Prefer simple data over rich types.\n115: - Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n116: - Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n117: - Avoid cleverness.\n118: - Name things after what they do to streams.\n119: \n120: ## Development Workflow\n121: \n122: - Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n123: - Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n124: - Do not optimize prematurely.\n125: - When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n126: - Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n127: - Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n128:   the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n129:   detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n130:   leaves to review.\n131: \n132: ### Problem Decomposition\n133: \n134: If a problem appears complex:\n135: 1. Decompose it into streams.\n136: 2. Identify invariants.\n137: 3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n138: 4. Make causality explicit.\n139: 5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n140: \n141: ## Operational & Specialized Guides\n142: \n143: - **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n144: - **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n145: - **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n146: - **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n147: - **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n148: "}],"_meta":{"codex/nodeReplExecutionDurationMs":5,"codex/telemetry":{"error_code":"unknown"}},"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"node_repl","tool":"js","arguments":{"code":"for (var p of ['src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj', 'src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc', 'docs/agents/build-n-test.md', '.cljstyle']) { try { var s = await fs.readFile(root + '/' + p, 'utf8'); nodeRepl.write(p + '\\n' + s.split('\\n').map((l,i) => `${i+1}: ${l}`).join('\\n')); } catch(e) { nodeRepl.write(p + ': ' + e.message); } }","title":"Read harness and owner-round implementation"},"result":null,"error":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"node_repl","tool":"js","arguments":{"code":"for (var p of ['src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj', 'src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc', 'docs/agents/build-n-test.md', '.cljstyle']) { try { var s = await fs.readFile(root + '/' + p, 'utf8'); nodeRepl.write(p + '\\n' + s.split('\\n').map((l,i) => `${i+1}: ${l}`).join('\\n')); } catch(e) { nodeRepl.write(p + ': ' + e.message); } }","title":"Read harness and owner-round implementation"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj\n1: (ns jolt-workload-w\n2:   \"J6g Workload W harness and peer cross-host integration (BRIEF-j6g.md).\n3:    Pre-registered criterion (v3 Section 2 A1, amended by v3.1 D4):\n4:    One jolt process serving two peers on the same machine while the REPL evaluates\n5:    a submitted loop each round; peer A sends one binary message at the\n6:    composition's :ws/max-frame-bytes (4,194,304 bytes); peer B exchanges 1 KiB\n7:    request/reply messages concurrently, up to 8 outstanding, at 10 Hz, for the\n8:    whole of A's transfer and >= 60 s (>= 600 overlapping round trips; fewer than\n9:    500 samples is an invalid run).\n10:    Criterion: during A's transfer, B's round-trip p99 <= 250 ms, no reply lost;\n11:    A's message delivered once; the REPL step count advances every owner round.\n12: \n13:    Deliverable 1: The workload W harness and sampler.\n14:    Deliverable 2: Peer cross-host rows (hosts that dial/listen, reachability\n15:    refusals on the REPL path).\n16:    Deliverable 3: REPL served-stream rejoin peer probe.\"\n17:   (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n18:             [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n19:             [dao.host.text :as text]\n20:             [jolt.io-poller :as poller]\n21:             [dao.stream :as stream]\n22:             [dao.stream.cbor :as cbor]\n23:             [dao.stream.remote-channel :as remote-channel]\n24:             [dao.stream.rpc :as rpc]\n25:             [dao.stream.waitset.driver :as wake]\n26:             [dao.stream.ws :as ws]\n27:             [dao.stream.ws.jolt :as wsj]\n28:             [dao.stream.ws.rfc6455 :as rfc6455]\n29:             [dao.stream.ws.tcp :as tcp]\n30:             [dao.stream.ws.upgrade :as upgrade]\n31:             [jolt.ffi :as ffi]\n32:             [jolt.socket.native :as native]\n33:             [yin.repl.connect :as connect]\n34:             [yin.repl.driver :as driver]\n35:             [yin.repl.host :as host]\n36:             [yin.repl.driver :as driver]\n37:             [yin.repl.main :as repl.main]\n38:             [yin.repl.serve :as serve]))\n39: \n40: \n41: ;; =============================================================================\n42: ;; Raw TCP helpers (probe sockets outside the adapter)\n43: ;; =============================================================================\n44: \n45: (defn- bvec\n46:   [bs]\n47:   (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 0xFF) (vec bs)))\n48: \n49: \n50: (defn- octets\n51:   [v]\n52:   (byte-array (map unchecked-byte v)))\n53: \n54: \n55: (defn- ascii\n56:   [v]\n57:   (apply str (map char v)))\n58: \n59: \n60: (defn- with-native\n61:   [n f]\n62:   (let [p (ffi/alloc n)]\n63:     (try (f p)\n64:          (finally (ffi/free p)))))\n65: \n66: \n67: (defn- raw-peer!\n68:   [port]\n69:   (let [{:keys [fd state]} (tcp/connect! (tcp/parse-address \"127.0.0.1\" port))]\n70:     (when (= :connecting state)\n71:       (native/poll-one fd native/pollout 2000)\n72:       (tcp/connect-result fd))\n73:     {:fd fd, :in (atom []), :end (atom nil)}))\n74: \n75: \n76: (defn- raw-poll!\n77:   [{:keys [fd in end]}]\n78:   (when-not @end\n79:     (with-native 65536\n80:       (fn [p]\n81:         (loop []\n82:           (let [r (tcp/recv-chunk! fd p 65536)]\n83:             (cond\n84:               (= :would-block r) nil\n85:               (= :eof r) (reset! end :eof)\n86:               (:error r) (reset! end (:class r))\n87:               :else (do (swap! in into (bvec (ffi/read-array p (:n r))))\n88:                         (recur)))))))))\n89: \n90: \n91: (defn- write-all!\n92:   [fd bs]\n93:   (let [bs (if (bytes/bytes? bs) bs (octets bs))\n94:         n (alength bs)\n95:         max-stalls 100]\n96:     (with-native n\n97:       (fn [p]\n98:         (ffi/write-array p bs)\n99:         (loop [off 0\n100:                stalls 0]\n101:           (if (>= off n)\n102:             off\n103:             (let [r (tcp/send-chunk! fd p (- n off))]\n104:               (cond\n105:                 (= :would-block r)\n106:                 (if (>= stalls max-stalls)\n107:                   (throw (ex-info \"raw write-all stalled on :would-block\"\n108:                                   {:off off :total n :stalls stalls}))\n109:                   (do (native/poll-one fd native/pollout 2000)\n110:                       (recur off (inc stalls))))\n111:                 (:error r) (throw (ex-info \"raw write failed\" {:error r}))\n112:                 :else (let [written (if (map? r) (:n r) (- n off))]\n113:                         (recur (+ off written) 0))))))))))\n114: \n115: \n116: (defn- write-some!\n117:   [fd bs off limit]\n118:   (let [bs (if (bytes/bytes? bs) bs (octets bs))\n119:         n (min (- (alength bs) off) limit)]\n120:     (with-native n\n121:       (fn [p]\n122:         (ffi/write-array p bs off n)\n123:         (let [r (tcp/send-chunk! fd p n)]\n124:           (cond\n125:             (= :would-block r) :would-block\n126:             (:error r) (throw (ex-info \"raw write-some failed\" {:error r}))\n127:             (map? r) (:n r)\n128:             :else n))))))\n129: \n130: \n131: (defn- head-end\n132:   [v]\n133:   (let [n (count v)]\n134:     (loop [i 3]\n135:       (cond\n136:         (>= i n) nil\n137:         (and (= 13 (nth v (- i 3)))\n138:              (= 10 (nth v (- i 2)))\n139:              (= 13 (nth v (- i 1)))\n140:              (= 10 (nth v i)))\n141:         (inc i)\n142:         :else (recur (inc i))))))\n143: \n144: \n145: (defn- raw-close!\n146:   [peer]\n147:   (tcp/shutdown! (:fd peer) :both)\n148:   (tcp/close! (:fd peer)))\n149: \n150: \n151: ;; =============================================================================\n152: ;; Int32 Big-Endian packing for request IDs\n153: ;; =============================================================================\n154: \n155: (defn- int32->bytes!\n156:   [bs off val]\n157:   (bytes/set! bs off (bit-and (bit-shift-right val 24) 0xFF))\n158:   (bytes/set! bs (+ off 1) (bit-and (bit-shift-right val 16) 0xFF))\n159:   (bytes/set! bs (+ off 2) (bit-and (bit-shift-right val 8) 0xFF))\n160:   (bytes/set! bs (+ off 3) (bit-and val 0xFF))\n161:   bs)\n162: \n163: \n164: (defn- bytes->int32\n165:   [bs off]\n166:   (bit-or (bit-shift-left (bit-and (bytes/get bs off) 0xFF) 24)\n167:           (bit-shift-left (bit-and (bytes/get bs (+ off 1)) 0xFF) 16)\n168:           (bit-shift-left (bit-and (bytes/get bs (+ off 2)) 0xFF) 8)\n169:           (bit-and (bytes/get bs (+ off 3)) 0xFF)))\n170: \n171: \n172: ;; =============================================================================\n173: ;; Deliverable 1: Workload W Harness\n174: ;; =============================================================================\n175: \n176: (defn- make-1kib-request\n177:   [id]\n178:   (let [bs (bytes/alloc 1024)]\n179:     (int32->bytes! bs 0 id)\n180:     bs))\n181: \n182: \n183: (defn run-workload-w\n184:   \"Run the pre-registered Workload W benchmark:\n185:    - Peer A: one 4,194,304 byte binary message (:ws/max-frame-bytes) paced over >= 60 s.\n186:    - Peer B: 1 KiB request/reply concurrent (up to 8 outstanding) at 10 Hz.\n187:    - REPL loop: evaluates a submitted loop each round, step count advances every round.\n188:    - Criterion: p99 <= 250 ms, no reply lost, A delivered once, REPL step advances every round.\n189:    Returns {:pass? ... :p99 ... :samples ... :delivered-once? ... :repl-advances? ...}.\"\n190:   [& {:keys [target-duration-ms target-samples]\n191:       :or {target-duration-ms 60000 target-samples 600}}]\n192:   (println \"================================================================================\")\n193:   (println \"J6g Workload W: Starting benchmark (target duration:\" target-duration-ms\n194:            \"ms, target samples:\" target-samples \")\")\n195:   (println \"================================================================================\")\n196:   (let [w (wake/make-wake)\n197:         server-inst (wsj/create)\n198:         client-b-inst (wsj/create)\n199: \n200:         ;; Peer A accounting\n201:         a-deliveries (atom [])\n202:         a-delivered-once? (atom false)\n203: \n204:         ;; Server bind\n205:         server-bound? (atom false)\n206:         server-port (atom 0)\n207:         _ (wsj/bind! server-inst\n208:                      {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n209:                       :bind-port 0\n210:                       :ws/max-frame-bytes 4194304\n211:                       :codecs [cbor/profile]\n212:                       :deposit! (fn [event detail]\n213:                                   (when (= :bind-succeeded event)\n214:                                     (reset! server-port (:port detail))\n215:                                     (reset! server-bound? true)))\n216:                       :accept! (fn [path seam _now]\n217:                                  (cond\n218:                                    (= path \"/peer-a\")\n219:                                    {:ws/adapter\n220:                                     {:opened! (fn [])\n221:                                      :binary! (fn [payload]\n222:                                                 (swap! a-deliveries conj (alength payload)))\n223:                                      :message! (fn [_])\n224:                                      :closed! (fn [_ _])\n225:                                      :error! (fn [_])\n226:                                      :too-large! (fn [])}}\n227: \n228:                                    (= path \"/peer-b\")\n229:                                    (let [seam-ref (atom seam)]\n230:                                      {:ws/adapter\n231:                                       {:opened! (fn [])\n232:                                        :binary! (fn [payload]\n233:                                                   ((:send! @seam-ref) payload))\n234:                                        :message! (fn [msg]\n235:                                                    ((:send! @seam-ref) msg))\n236:                                        :closed! (fn [_ _])\n237:                                        :error! (fn [_])\n238:                                        :too-large! (fn [])}})))})\n239: \n240:         ;; Drive until server listener is bound\n241:         _ (loop []\n242:             (when-not @server-bound?\n243:               (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump server-inst) nil)\n244:               (recur)))\n245:         port @server-port]\n246: \n247:     (println \"Server listening on port\" port)\n248: \n249:     ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n250:     ;; Peer B setup: connects via client adapter\n251:     ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n252:     (let [b-opened? (atom false)\n253:           b-samples (atom [])\n254:           b-outstanding (atom {})\n255:           b-lost (atom 0)\n256:           b-req-seq (atom 0)\n257:           b-seam (atom nil)\n258:           b-cb {:ws/codec cbor/profile\n259:                 :ws/max-frame-bytes 4194304\n260:                 :opened! #(reset! b-opened? true)\n261:                 :binary! (fn [payload]\n262:                            (let [now (poller/clock-ms)\n263:                                  req-id (bytes->int32 payload 0)\n264:                                  sent-at (get @b-outstanding req-id)]\n265:                              (if sent-at\n266:                                (do (swap! b-outstanding dissoc req-id)\n267:                                    (swap! b-samples conj (- now sent-at)))\n268:                                (swap! b-lost inc))))\n269:                 :message! (fn [_])\n270:                 :closed! (fn [_ _])\n271:                 :error! (fn [_])\n272:                 :too-large! (fn [])}]\n273: \n274:       (reset! b-seam (wsj/connect! client-b-inst\n275:                                    {:ws/host \"127.0.0.1\"\n276:                                     :ws/port port\n277:                                     :ws/path \"/peer-b\"}\n278:                                    b-cb))\n279: \n280:       ;; Drive until Peer B connection is open\n281:       (loop []\n282:         (when-not @b-opened?\n283:           (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump server-inst) nil)\n284:           (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump client-b-inst) nil)\n285:           (recur)))\n286: \n287:       (println \"Peer B connected and upgraded\")\n288: \n289:       ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n290:       ;; Peer A setup: connects via raw socket, upgrades to /peer-a\n291:       ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n292:       (let [raw-a (raw-peer! port)\n293:             upgrade-req (str \"GET /peer-a HTTP/1.1\\r\\n\"\n294:                              \"Host: 127.0.0.1:\" port \"\\r\\n\"\n295:                              \"Upgrade: websocket\\r\\n\"\n296:                              \"Connection: Upgrade\\r\\n\"\n297:                              \"Sec-WebSocket-Key: dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ==\\r\\n\"\n298:                              \"Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13\\r\\n\"\n299:                              \"Sec-WebSocket-Protocol: dao.stream.cbor\\r\\n\\r\\n\")]\n300:         (write-all! (:fd raw-a) (text/utf8-encode upgrade-req))\n301: \n302:         ;; Drive until Peer A upgrade response arrives\n303:         (loop []\n304:           (raw-poll! raw-a)\n305:           (when-not (head-end @(:in raw-a))\n306:             (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump server-inst) nil)\n307:             (recur)))\n308: \n309:         (println \"Peer A connected and upgraded\")\n310: \n311:         ;; Prepare Peer A's 4,194,304-byte binary frame\n312:         (let [a-payload (bytes/alloc 4194304)\n313:               mask-key (byte-array [(unchecked-byte 0x37) (unchecked-byte 0xFA)\n314:                                     (unchecked-byte 0x21) (unchecked-byte 0x3D)])\n315:               a-frame (rfc6455/encode-frame {:opcode :binary\n316:                                              :payload a-payload\n317:                                              :mask-key mask-key\n318:                                              :fin? true})\n319:               total-a-frame-len (alength a-frame)\n320:               a-sent (atom 0)\n321: \n322:               ;; REPL loop setup\n323:               repl-state (atom (repl.main/boot {:adapter (host/websocket)}))\n324:               repl-step-count (atom 0)\n325:               owner-round-count (atom 0)\n326: \n327:               ;; Timing and dispatch state\n328:               start-time (poller/clock-ms)\n329:               last-10hz-tick (atom start-time)\n330:               last-telemetry-tick (atom start-time)\n331:               a-stalls (atom 0)\n332:               a-stalled? (atom false)\n333:               timed-out? (atom false)\n334:               hard-timeout-ms 300000\n335:               max-consecutive-stalls 500\n336:               server-pump (wsj/host-pump server-inst)\n337:               client-b-pump (wsj/host-pump client-b-inst)]\n338: \n339:           (println \"Frame length for Peer A:\" total-a-frame-len \"bytes\")\n340:           (println \"Entering owner round loop...\")\n341: \n342:           ;; Main owner loop\n343:           (loop []\n344:             (let [now (poller/clock-ms)\n345:                   elapsed (- now start-time)]\n346: \n347:               ;; Hard timeout guard: cap whole run at 5 minutes\n348:               (if (>= elapsed hard-timeout-ms)\n349:                 (do (reset! timed-out? true)\n350:                     (println \"HARD CAP EXCEEDED: workload-w exceeded 5 minutes (\" elapsed \"ms), terminating loop.\"))\n351: \n352:                 (do\n353:                   ;; 1. Advance owner round and step REPL loop\n354:                   (swap! owner-round-count inc)\n355:                   (driver/submit-line! (:input @repl-state)\n356:                                        \"(loop [i 0] (if (< i 5) (recur (inc i)) i))\")\n357:                   (let [stepped (driver/repl-step @repl-state now)\n358:                         [_entries next-st] (driver/take-outbox stepped)]\n359:                     (reset! repl-state next-st)\n360:                     (swap! repl-step-count inc))\n361: \n362:                   ;; 2. Paced Peer A transfer across target-duration-ms (with no-progress guard)\n363:                   (when-not @a-stalled?\n364:                     (let [target-bytes (if (>= elapsed target-duration-ms)\n365:                                          total-a-frame-len\n366:                                          (min total-a-frame-len\n367:                                               (long (* total-a-frame-len\n368:                                                        (/ (double elapsed) (double target-duration-ms))))))]\n369:                       (when (< @a-sent target-bytes)\n370:                         (let [to-write (min (- target-bytes @a-sent) 65536)\n371:                               r (write-some! (:fd raw-a) a-frame @a-sent to-write)]\n372:                           (cond\n373:                             (= :would-block r)\n374:                             (let [stalls (swap! a-stalls inc)]\n375:                               (when (>= stalls max-consecutive-stalls)\n376:                                 (reset! a-stalled? true)\n377:                                 (println \"TRANSFER STALLED: Peer A hit\" stalls\n378:                                          \"consecutive :would-block polls at\" @a-sent \"/\" total-a-frame-len \"bytes. Aborting transfer leg.\")))\n379: \n380:                             (and (number? r) (pos? r))\n381:                             (do (reset! a-stalls 0)\n382:                                 (swap! a-sent + r))\n383: \n384:                             :else\n385:                             (swap! a-stalls inc))))))\n386: \n387:                   ;; 3. Peer B 10 Hz dispatch: concurrent requests up to 8 outstanding\n388:                   (when (>= (- now @last-10hz-tick) 100)\n389:                     (reset! last-10hz-tick now)\n390:                     (when (< (count @b-outstanding) 8)\n391:                       (let [id (swap! b-req-seq inc)\n392:                             req-bs (make-1kib-request id)]\n393:                         (swap! b-outstanding assoc id now)\n394:                         ((:send! @b-seam) req-bs))))\n395: \n396:                   ;; 4. Periodic sampler flush every 10 seconds to ensure telemetry\n397:                   (when (>= (- now @last-telemetry-tick) 10000)\n398:                     (reset! last-telemetry-tick now)\n399:                     (let [sc (count @b-samples)\n400:                           cur-p99 (if (pos? sc)\n401:                                     (nth (sort @b-samples) (min (dec sc) (int (* 0.99 sc))))\n402:                                     0)]\n403:                       (println (str \"TELEMETRY: elapsed=\" (long (/ elapsed 1000)) \"s, samples=\" sc\n404:                                     \", running p99=\" cur-p99 \"ms, A-sent=\" @a-sent \"/\" total-a-frame-len\n405:                                     \" (\" (long (* 100 (/ (double @a-sent) (double total-a-frame-len)))) \"%), A-deliveries=\" (count @a-deliveries)\n406:                                     (when @a-stalled? \" [A-STALLED]\")))\n407:                       (flush)))\n408: \n409:                   ;; 5. Pump I/O for server and client B\n410:                   (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 server-pump nil)\n411:                   (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 client-b-pump nil)\n412: \n413:                   ;; Check exit conditions\n414:                   (let [a-done? (or @a-stalled?\n415:                                     (and (>= @a-sent total-a-frame-len)\n416:                                          (seq @a-deliveries)))\n417:                         time-done? (>= elapsed target-duration-ms)\n418:                         samples-done? (>= (count @b-samples) target-samples)]\n419: \n420:                     (if (and a-done? time-done? samples-done?)\n421:                       ;; Drain trailing in-flight requests briefly\n422:                       (let [drain-deadline (+ (poller/clock-ms) 500)]\n423:                         (loop []\n424:                           (when (and (seq @b-outstanding)\n425:                                      (< (poller/clock-ms) drain-deadline))\n426:                             (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 server-pump nil)\n427:                             (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 client-b-pump nil)\n428:                             (recur))))\n429:                       (recur)))))))\n430: \n431:           ;; Cleanup sockets\n432:           (raw-close! raw-a)\n433:           (wsj/close-host! client-b-inst)\n434:           (wsj/close-host! server-inst)\n435:           (wake/close! w)\n436: \n437:           ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------\n438:           ;; Evaluation of pre-registered criterion\n439:           ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------\n440:           (let [sample-count (count @b-samples)\n441:                 valid-run? (>= sample-count 500)\n442:                 target-samples-met? (>= sample-count target-samples)\n443:                 sorted (sort @b-samples)\n444:                 p99-idx (int (* 0.99 sample-count))\n445:                 p99 (if (pos? sample-count)\n446:                       (nth sorted (min (dec sample-count) p99-idx))\n447:                       0)\n448:                 p99-ok? (<= p99 250)\n449:                 repl-advances? (= @owner-round-count @repl-step-count)\n450:                 a-deliv-count (count @a-deliveries)\n451:                 a-deliv-len (or (first @a-deliveries) 0)\n452:                 delivered-once? (and (= 1 a-deliv-count)\n453:                                      (= 4194304 a-deliv-len))\n454:                 replies-lost (count @b-outstanding)\n455:                 no-reply-lost? (and (zero? @b-lost) (zero? replies-lost))\n456:                 all-pass? (and (not @timed-out?)\n457:                                (not @a-stalled?)\n458:                                valid-run?\n459:                                target-samples-met?\n460:                                p99-ok?\n461:                                repl-advances?\n462:                                delivered-once?\n463:                                no-reply-lost?)]\n464: \n465:             (println)\n466:             (println \"================================================================================\")\n467:             (println \"Workload W Results:\" (if all-pass? \"SUCCESS\" \"FAILURE\"))\n468:             (println \"================================================================================\")\n469:             (println \"  Hard cap timed out:           \" @timed-out?)\n470:             (println \"  Peer A transfer stalled:      \" @a-stalled?)\n471:             (println \"  Sample count:                 \" sample-count\n472:                      (str \"(valid >= 500: \" valid-run? \", target >= \" target-samples \": \" target-samples-met? \")\"))\n473:             (println \"  p99 round-trip latency:       \" (if (pos? sample-count) (str p99 \"ms\") \"ABSENT\")\n474:                      (str \"(criterion <= 250 ms: \" p99-ok? \")\"))\n475:             (println \"  Peer B replies lost:          \" replies-lost \"(unanswered) +\" @b-lost \"(corrupt)\")\n476:             (println \"  Peer A delivered once:        \" delivered-once?\n477:                      (str \"(deliveries: \" a-deliv-count \", bytes: \" a-deliv-len \", sent: \" @a-sent \")\"))\n478:             (println \"  REPL per-round step advance:  \" repl-advances?\n479:                      (str \"(rounds: \" @owner-round-count \", REPL steps: \" @repl-step-count \")\"))\n480:             (println)\n481: \n482:             ;; Required report records per BRIEF-j6g.md\n483:             (if (pos? sample-count)\n484:               (println \"RECORD p99\" p99 \"ms\")\n485:               (println \"RECORD p99 ABSENT\"))\n486:             (println \"RECORD sample count\" sample-count)\n487:             (println \"RECORD delivered once\" delivered-once?)\n488:             (println \"RECORD per-round step advance\" repl-advances?)\n489: \n490:             (if all-pass?\n491:               (do (println \"PASS workload-w\")\n492:                   {:pass? true\n493:                    :p99 p99\n494:                    :samples sample-count\n495:                    :delivered-once? delivered-once?\n496:                    :repl-advances? repl-advances?\n497:                    :replies-lost replies-lost})\n498:               (do (println \"FAIL workload-w\")\n499:                   {:pass? false\n500:                    :p99 p99\n501:                    :samples sample-count\n502:                    :delivered-once? delivered-once?\n503:                    :repl-advances? repl-advances?\n504:                    :replies-lost replies-lost}))))))))\n505: \n506: \n507: ;; =============================================================================\n508: ;; Deliverable 2: Peer Cross-Host Rows\n509: ;; =============================================================================\n510: \n511: (defn run-cross-host-rows\n512:   \"Verify cross-host listener, dialer, and REPL path reachability rows:\n513:    - Hosts that listen: 127.0.0.1, 0.0.0.0, localhost, ::1; port 0 ephemeral; port collision.\n514:    - Hosts that dial: 127.0.0.1, localhost, [::1]; hostname refused; wss refused.\n515:    - REPL reachability refusals: local reachability failure, reachability failure, disclaimed path.\"\n516:   []\n517:   (println \"================================================================================\")\n518:   (println \"J6g Cross-Host Rows: Running checks...\")\n519:   (println \"================================================================================\")\n520:   (let [w (wake/make-wake)\n521:         passed (atom 0)\n522:         failed (atom 0)\n523:         check! (fn [label ok? detail]\n524:                  (if ok?\n525:                    (do (swap! passed inc)\n526:                        (println \"  ok  \" label))\n527:                    (do (swap! failed inc)\n528:                        (println \"  FAIL\" label detail))))]\n529: \n530:     ;; 1. Hosts that listen: 127.0.0.1, 0.0.0.0, localhost, ::1\n531:     (doseq [bind-host [\"127.0.0.1\" \"0.0.0.0\" \"localhost\" \"::1\"]]\n532:       (let [inst (wsj/create)\n533:             bound-port (atom nil)\n534:             res (wsj/bind! inst {:bind-host bind-host\n535:                                  :bind-port 0\n536:                                  :deposit! (fn [ev detail]\n537:                                              (when (= :bind-succeeded ev)\n538:                                                (reset! bound-port (:port detail))))})]\n539:         (check! (str \"listen on \" bind-host \" binds ephemeral port\")\n540:                 (and (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome res))\n541:                      (pos? (or @bound-port 0)))\n542:                 {:res res :port @bound-port})\n543:         (wsj/close-host! inst)))\n544: \n545:     ;; Port collision check\n546:     (let [inst1 (wsj/create)\n547:           inst2 (wsj/create)\n548:           bound-port (atom nil)\n549:           _ (wsj/bind! inst1 {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n550:                               :bind-port 0\n551:                               :deposit! (fn [ev detail]\n552:                                           (when (= :bind-succeeded ev)\n553:                                             (reset! bound-port (:port detail))))})\n554:           collision-res (wsj/bind! inst2 {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n555:                                           :bind-port @bound-port\n556:                                           :deposit! (fn [_ _])})]\n557:       (check! \"listen on colliding port returns transport-error\"\n558:               (= :dao.stream/transport-error (:dao.stream/outcome collision-res))\n559:               {:collision collision-res})\n560:       (wsj/close-host! inst2)\n561:       (wsj/close-host! inst1))\n562: \n563:     ;; 2. Hosts that dial: 127.0.0.1, localhost, [::1]\n564:     (doseq [[bind-h dial-h label] [[\"127.0.0.1\" \"127.0.0.1\" \"127.0.0.1\"]\n565:                                    [\"127.0.0.1\" \"localhost\" \"localhost\"]\n566:                                    [\"::1\" \"[::1]\" \"[::1]\"]]]\n567:       (let [si (wsj/create)\n568:             ci (wsj/create)\n569:             port (atom nil)\n570:             _ (wsj/bind! si {:bind-host bind-h\n571:                              :bind-port 0\n572:                              :deposit! (fn [ev detail]\n573:                                          (when (= :bind-succeeded ev)\n574:                                            (reset! port (:port detail))))})\n575:             opened? (atom false)\n576:             seam (wsj/connect! ci\n577:                                {:ws/host dial-h :ws/port @port :ws/path \"/repl\"}\n578:                                {:ws/codec cbor/profile\n579:                                 :opened! #(reset! opened? true)\n580:                                 :binary! (fn [_])\n581:                                 :message! (fn [_])\n582:                                 :closed! (fn [_ _])\n583:                                 :error! (fn [_])\n584:                                 :too-large! (fn [])})\n585:             deadline (+ (poller/clock-ms) 5000)]\n586:         (loop []\n587:           (when-not (or @opened? (> (poller/clock-ms) deadline))\n588:             (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump si) nil)\n589:             (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump ci) nil)\n590:             (recur)))\n591:         (check! (str \"dial on \" label \" connects and opens\")\n592:                 (and (some? seam) @opened?)\n593:                 {:seam seam :opened? @opened?})\n594:         (wsj/close-host! ci)\n595:         (wsj/close-host! si)))\n596: \n597:     ;; Refusal of host name (resolver trap not sprung)\n598:     (let [ci (wsj/create)\n599:           trap (fn [& _] (throw (ex-info \"resolver called\" {})))]\n600:       (with-redefs-fn {#'native/resolve-addrs trap\n601:                        #'native/c-getaddrinfo trap}\n602:         (fn []\n603:           (let [seam (wsj/connect! ci\n604:                                    {:ws/host \"example.invalid\"\n605:                                     :ws/port 80\n606:                                     :ws/path \"/repl\"}\n607:                                    {:opened! (fn [])\n608:                                     :closed! (fn [_ _])\n609:                                     :message! (fn [_])\n610:                                     :binary! (fn [_])})]\n611:             (check! \"dial to hostname returns nil, resolver trap not sprung\"\n612:                     (nil? seam)\n613:                     {:seam seam}))))\n614:       (wsj/close-host! ci))\n615: \n616:     ;; Refusal of wss scheme\n617:     (let [ci (wsj/create)\n618:           seam (wsj/connect! ci\n619:                                {:ws/scheme \"wss\"\n620:                                 :ws/host \"127.0.0.1\"\n621:                                 :ws/port 443\n622:                                 :ws/path \"/repl\"}\n623:                                {:opened! (fn [])\n624:                                 :closed! (fn [_ _])\n625:                                 :message! (fn [_])\n626:                                 :binary! (fn [_])})]\n627:       (check! \"dial with wss scheme is refused explicitly (nil returned)\"\n628:               (nil? seam)\n629:               {:seam seam})\n630:       (wsj/close-host! ci))\n631: \n632:     ;; 3. Reachability refusals on the REPL path (yin.repl.connect/open)\n633:     ;; 3a. Host name yields local reachability failure text\n634:     (let [ci (wsj/create)\n635:           opened (connect/open {:url \"daostream:ws://example.invalid:80/repl\"\n636:                                 :host {:connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! ci d a))}\n637:                                 :now 0})\n638:           text (pr-str opened)]\n639:       (check! \"connect/open to hostname gives local reachability failure text\"\n640:               (str/includes? text \"local reachability failure\")\n641:               {:opened text})\n642:       (wsj/close-host! ci))\n643: \n644:     ;; 3b. Unbound / unreachable port yields reachability failure text\n645:     (let [ci (wsj/create)\n646:           ;; Unbound port on loopback: dial fails immediately on connect\n647:           unused-port 59999\n648:           opened (connect/open {:url (str \"daostream:ws://127.0.0.1:\" unused-port \"/repl\")\n649:                                 :host (host/websocket)\n650:                                 :now 0})\n651:           conn (connect/connection-key opened)\n652:           client (connect/client-key opened)\n653:           deadline (+ (poller/clock-ms) 3000)\n654:           observed (loop [c conn cl client]\n655:                      (let [c' (connect/step! c (poller/clock-ms))\n656:                            cl' (:dao.stream.rpc/state (rpc/poll! cl))\n657:                            [c'' ev] (connect/observe-terminal c' (:terminal cl'))]\n658:                        (cond\n659:                          ev [c'' ev]\n660:                          (> (poller/clock-ms) deadline) [c'' nil]\n661:                          :else (do (wake/sleep-fds! w 10 nil nil)\n662:                                    (recur c'' cl')))))\n663:           event (second observed)\n664:           ev-text (connect/text-key event)]\n665:       (check! \"connect/open to unbound port observes transport-error reachability failure\"\n666:               (and (some? event)\n667:                    (str/includes? (str ev-text) \"reachability failure\"))\n668:               {:event event :text ev-text})\n669:       (wsj/close-host! ci))\n670: \n671:     (wake/close! w)\n672:     (println)\n673:     (println \"Cross-host rows:\" @passed \"passed,\" @failed \"failed\")\n674:     (if (zero? @failed)\n675:       (do (println \"PASS cross-host\") true)\n676:       (do (println \"FAIL cross-host\") false))))\n677: \n678: \n679: ;; =============================================================================\n680: ;; Deliverable 3: REPL Served-Stream Rejoin Checks\n681: ;; =============================================================================\n682: \n683: (defn- step-peer!\n684:   [peer now]\n685:   (swap! (:connection peer) connect/step! now)\n686:   (swap! (:client peer) #(:dao.stream.rpc/state (rpc/poll! %))))\n687: \n688: \n689: (defn- drive-repl-peers!\n690:   [w insts peers pred ms]\n691:   (let [deadline (+ (poller/clock-ms) ms)\n692:         hps (mapv wsj/host-pump insts)]\n693:     (loop []\n694:       (let [now (poller/clock-ms)]\n695:         (doseq [p peers] (step-peer! p now))\n696:         (doseq [hp hps] (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 hp nil))\n697:         (cond\n698:           (pred) true\n699:           (> (poller/clock-ms) deadline) false\n700:           :else (recur))))))\n701: \n702: \n703: (defn run-served-stream-rejoin\n704:   \"Run the 4 served-stream facts over two jolt host instances:\n705:    Fact 1: evaluation round trips and two clients get their own answers.\n706:    Fact 2: killing connection is observable and requests are lost.\n707:    Fact 3: reattaching resumes served stream and deposit medium.\n708:    Fact 4: stopping endpoint ends served stream (not closes it).\"\n709:   []\n710:   (println \"================================================================================\")\n711:   (println \"J6g Served-Stream Rejoin: Running 4 served-stream peer facts...\")\n712:   (println \"================================================================================\")\n713:   (let [w (wake/make-wake)\n714:         passed (atom 0)\n715:         failed (atom 0)\n716:         check! (fn [label ok? detail]\n717:                  (if ok?\n718:                    (do (swap! passed inc)\n719:                        (println \"  ok  \" label))\n720:                    (do (swap! failed inc)\n721:                        (println \"  FAIL\" label detail))))]\n722: \n723:     ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n724:     ;; Fact 1: Two clients connect and get their own answers without crosstalk\n725:     ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n726:     (let [si (wsj/create)\n727:           ci1 (wsj/create)\n728:           ci2 (wsj/create)\n729:           port (atom 0)\n730:           server-st (atom (serve/serve! {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n731:                                          :bind-port 0\n732:                                          :host {:bind! (fn [opts]\n733:                                                          (wsj/bind! si (assoc opts :ws.jolt/adapter-of\n734:                                                                               #(some-> (:ws/handle %) ws/adapter))))\n735:                                                 :unbind! (fn [lh dep] (wsj/unbind! si lh dep))\n736:                                                 :connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! si d a))}}))\n737:           ;; Advance server until port bound\n738:           _ (loop []\n739:               (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n740:                     [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n741:                 (reset! server-st next-st)\n742:                 (when-let [p (get-in next-st [:spec :port])]\n743:                   (when (pos? p) (reset! port p)))\n744:                 (when (zero? @port)\n745:                   (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump si) nil)\n746:                   (recur))))\n747:           url (str \"daostream:ws://127.0.0.1:\" @port \"/repl\")\n748:           now (poller/clock-ms)\n749:           p1-open (connect/open {:url url :host {:connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! ci1 d a))} :now now})\n750:           p2-open (connect/open {:url url :host {:connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! ci2 d a))} :now now})\n751:           peer1 {:connection (atom (connect/connection-key p1-open))\n752:                  :client (atom (connect/client-key p1-open))}\n753:           peer2 {:connection (atom (connect/connection-key p2-open))\n754:                  :client (atom (connect/client-key p2-open))}]\n755: \n756:       ;; Send eval requests\n757:       (let [c1 (rpc/request! @(:client peer1) 1 :op/eval \"(+ 40 2)\")\n758:             c2 (rpc/request! @(:client peer2) 2 :op/eval \"(+ 20 3)\")]\n759:         (reset! (:client peer1) c1)\n760:         (reset! (:client peer2) c2)\n761: \n762:         ;; Drive until both answers arrive\n763:         (drive-repl-peers! w [si ci1 ci2] [peer1 peer2]\n764:                            (fn []\n765:                              (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n766:                                    [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n767:                                (reset! server-st next-st)\n768:                                (and (some? (rpc/response @(:client peer1) 1))\n769:                                     (some? (rpc/response @(:client peer2) 2)))))\n770:                            5000)\n771: \n772:         (let [ans1 (rpc/response @(:client peer1) 1)\n773:               ans2 (rpc/response @(:client peer2) 2)]\n774:           (check! \"Fact 1: peer 1 gets 42 and peer 2 gets 23 without crosstalk\"\n775:                   (and (= \"42\" (rpc/answer-ok ans1))\n776:                        (= \"23\" (rpc/answer-ok ans2))\n777:                        (nil? (rpc/response @(:client peer1) 2))\n778:                        (nil? (rpc/response @(:client peer2) 1)))\n779:                   {:ans1 ans1 :ans2 ans2})))\n780: \n781:       (wsj/close-host! ci1)\n782:       (wsj/close-host! ci2)\n783:       (wsj/close-host! si))\n784: \n785:     ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n786:     ;; Fact 2: Killing connection while request in-flight observes request lost\n787:     ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n788:     (let [si (wsj/create)\n789:           ci (wsj/create)\n790:           port (atom 0)\n791:           server-st (atom (serve/serve! {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n792:                                          :bind-port 0\n793:                                          :host {:bind! (fn [opts]\n794:                                                          (wsj/bind! si (assoc opts :ws.jolt/adapter-of\n795:                                                                               #(some-> (:ws/handle %) ws/adapter))))\n796:                                                 :unbind! (fn [lh dep] (wsj/unbind! si lh dep))\n797:                                                 :connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! si d a))}}))\n798:           _ (loop []\n799:               (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n800:                     [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n801:                 (reset! server-st next-st)\n802:                 (when-let [p (get-in next-st [:spec :port])]\n803:                   (when (pos? p) (reset! port p)))\n804:                 (when (zero? @port)\n805:                   (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump si) nil)\n806:                   (recur))))\n807:           url (str \"daostream:ws://127.0.0.1:\" @port \"/repl\")\n808:           now (poller/clock-ms)\n809:           p-open (connect/open {:url url :host {:connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! ci d a))} :now now})\n810:           peer {:connection (atom (connect/connection-key p-open))\n811:                 :client (atom (connect/client-key p-open))}]\n812: \n813:       ;; Connect first\n814:       (drive-repl-peers! w [si ci] [peer]\n815:                          (fn []\n816:                            (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n817:                                  [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n818:                              (reset! server-st next-st)\n819:                              (remote-channel/opened? (:dial @(:connection peer)))))\n820:                          3000)\n821: \n822:       ;; Stall server driver, send request, and close client connection\n823:       (let [c (rpc/request! @(:client peer) 99 :op/eval \"(+ 1 2)\")]\n824:         (reset! (:client peer) c)\n825:         (swap! (:connection peer) connect/close!)\n826: \n827:         ;; Drive client until terminal observed\n828:         (drive-repl-peers! w [ci] [peer]\n829:                            (fn []\n830:                              (some? (:terminal @(:client peer))))\n831:                            3000)\n832: \n833:         (let [[conn' ev] (connect/observe-terminal @(:connection peer) (:terminal @(:client peer)))]\n834:           (check! \"Fact 2: client observes detached and lost request\"\n835:                   (and (= :detached (:status conn'))\n836:                        (= :dao.stream.rpc/detached (:terminal @(:client peer))))\n837:                   {:conn conn' :ev ev :terminal (:terminal @(:client peer))})))\n838: \n839:       (wsj/close-host! ci)\n840:       (wsj/close-host! si))\n841: \n842:     ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n843:     ;; Fact 3: Reattaching resumes served stream and shared shell state survives\n844:     ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n845:     (let [si (wsj/create)\n846:           ci (wsj/create)\n847:           port (atom 0)\n848:           server-st (atom (serve/serve! {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n849:                                          :bind-port 0\n850:                                          :host {:bind! (fn [opts]\n851:                                                          (wsj/bind! si (assoc opts :ws.jolt/adapter-of\n852:                                                                               #(some-> (:ws/handle %) ws/adapter))))\n853:                                                 :unbind! (fn [lh dep] (wsj/unbind! si lh dep))\n854:                                                 :connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! si d a))}}))\n855:           _ (loop []\n856:               (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n857:                     [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n858:                 (reset! server-st next-st)\n859:                 (when-let [p (get-in next-st [:spec :port])]\n860:                   (when (pos? p) (reset! port p)))\n861:                 (when (zero? @port)\n862:                   (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump si) nil)\n863:                   (recur))))\n864:           url (str \"daostream:ws://127.0.0.1:\" @port \"/repl\")\n865:           now (poller/clock-ms)\n866:           client-host {:connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! ci d a))}\n867:           p-open (connect/open {:url url :host client-host :now now})\n868:           peer {:connection (atom (connect/connection-key p-open))\n869:                 :client (atom (connect/client-key p-open))}]\n870: \n871:       ;; 1. Define x = 10\n872:       (let [c (rpc/request! @(:client peer) 10 :op/eval \"(def x 10)\")]\n873:         (reset! (:client peer) c)\n874:         (drive-repl-peers! w [si ci] [peer]\n875:                            (fn []\n876:                              (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n877:                                    [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n878:                                (reset! server-st next-st)\n879:                                (some? (rpc/response @(:client peer) 10))))\n880:                            5000)\n881:         (check! \"Fact 3: (def x 10) evaluated\"\n882:                 (= \"10\" (rpc/answer-ok (rpc/response @(:client peer) 10)))\n883:                 {:ans (rpc/response @(:client peer) 10)}))\n884: \n885:       ;; 2. Disconnect and reattach\n886:       (swap! (:connection peer) connect/close!)\n887:       (drive-repl-peers! w [ci] [peer]\n888:                          (fn [] (some? (:terminal @(:client peer))))\n889:                          2000)\n890:       (let [now' (poller/clock-ms)\n891:             reattached (connect/reattach @(:connection peer) @(:client peer) now')]\n892:         (reset! (:connection peer) (connect/connection-key reattached))\n893:         (reset! (:client peer) (connect/client-key reattached))\n894: \n895:         ;; 3. Query x on reattached connection\n896:         (let [c2 (rpc/request! @(:client peer) 11 :op/eval \"x\")]\n897:           (reset! (:client peer) c2)\n898:           (drive-repl-peers! w [si ci] [peer]\n899:                              (fn []\n900:                                (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n901:                                      [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n902:                                  (reset! server-st next-st)\n903:                                  (some? (rpc/response @(:client peer) 11))))\n904:                              5000)\n905:           (check! \"Fact 3: reattached connection reads x = 10\"\n906:                   (= \"10\" (rpc/answer-ok (rpc/response @(:client peer) 11)))\n907:                   {:ans (rpc/response @(:client peer) 11)})))\n908: \n909:       (wsj/close-host! ci)\n910:       (wsj/close-host! si))\n911: \n912:     ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n913:     ;; Fact 4: Stopping endpoint ends served stream (terminal :ended, not :detached)\n914:     ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n915:     (let [si (wsj/create)\n916:           ci (wsj/create)\n917:           port (atom 0)\n918:           server-st (atom (serve/serve! {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n919:                                          :bind-port 0\n920:                                          :host {:bind! (fn [opts]\n921:                                                          (wsj/bind! si (assoc opts :ws.jolt/adapter-of\n922:                                                                               #(some-> (:ws/handle %) ws/adapter))))\n923:                                                 :unbind! (fn [lh dep] (wsj/unbind! si lh dep))\n924:                                                 :connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! si d a))}}))\n925:           _ (loop []\n926:               (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n927:                     [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n928:                 (reset! server-st next-st)\n929:                 (when-let [p (get-in next-st [:spec :port])]\n930:                   (when (pos? p) (reset! port p)))\n931:                 (when (zero? @port)\n932:                   (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump si) nil)\n933:                   (recur))))\n934:           url (str \"daostream:ws://127.0.0.1:\" @port \"/repl\")\n935:           now (poller/clock-ms)\n936:           client-host {:connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! ci d a))}\n937:           p-open (connect/open {:url url :host client-host :now now})\n938:           peer {:connection (atom (connect/connection-key p-open))\n939:                 :client (atom (connect/client-key p-open))}]\n940: \n941:       ;; Open connection and eval (+ 3 4)\n942:       (let [c (rpc/request! @(:client peer) 7 :op/eval \"(+ 3 4)\")]\n943:         (reset! (:client peer) c)\n944:         (drive-repl-peers! w [si ci] [peer]\n945:                            (fn []\n946:                              (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n947:                                    [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n948:                                (reset! server-st next-st)\n949:                                (some? (rpc/response @(:client peer) 7))))\n950:                            5000)\n951:         (check! \"Fact 4: (+ 3 4) evaluates to 7\"\n952:                 (= \"7\" (rpc/answer-ok (rpc/response @(:client peer) 7)))\n953:                 {:ans (rpc/response @(:client peer) 7)}))\n954: \n955:       ;; Stop endpoint\n956:       (swap! server-st serve/stop!)\n957:       (drive-repl-peers! w [si ci] [peer]\n958:                          (fn []\n959:                            (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n960:                                  [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n961:                              (reset! server-st next-st)\n962:                              (and (serve/stopped? next-st)\n963:                                   (some? (:terminal @(:client peer))))))\n964:                          5000)\n965: \n966:       (let [[conn' ev] (connect/observe-terminal @(:connection peer) (:terminal @(:client peer)))]\n967:         (check! \"Fact 4: client observes :ended, not bare detached\"\n968:                 (and (= :ended (:status conn'))\n969:                      (= :dao.stream.rpc/ended (:terminal @(:client peer))))\n970:                 {:conn conn' :ev ev :terminal (:terminal @(:client peer))}))\n971: \n972:       (wsj/close-host! ci)\n973:       (wsj/close-host! si))\n974: \n975:     (wake/close! w)\n976:     (println)\n977:     (println \"Served-stream rejoin:\" @passed \"passed,\" @failed \"failed\")\n978:     (if (zero? @failed)\n979:       (do (println \"PASS served-stream\") true)\n980:       (do (println \"FAIL served-stream\") false))))\n981: \n982: \n983: ;; =============================================================================\n984: ;; CLI Dispatcher\n985: ;; =============================================================================\n986: \n987: (defn -main\n988:   [& args]\n989:   (let [cmd (or (first args) \"workload-w\")\n990:         ok? (case cmd\n991:               \"workload-w\" (:pass? (run-workload-w))\n992:               \"quick-w\" (:pass? (run-workload-w :target-duration-ms 2000 :target-samples 20))\n993:               \"cross-host\" (run-cross-host-rows)\n994:               \"served-stream\" (run-served-stream-rejoin)\n995:               \"all\" (and (run-cross-host-rows)\n996:                          (run-served-stream-rejoin)\n997:                          (:pass? (run-workload-w)))\n998:               (do (println \"Unknown command:\" cmd\n999:                            \"- valid commands: workload-w, quick-w, cross-host, served-stream, all\")\n1000:                   false))]\n1001:     (System/exit (if ok? 0 1))))\n1002: \n1003: (apply -main *command-line-args*)\n1004: src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc\n1: (ns yin.repl.main\n2:   \"Host entry points for the DaoStream Yin REPL.\n3: \n4:    The one thing this namespace adds to `yin.repl.driver` is cadence, which\n5:    the DaoStream contract leaves to the runtime.  Per host, a line producer\n6:    appends what was typed to the input medium, nudges the step owner's wake\n7:    source, and returns; one ticker owns the state value and calls\n8:    `repl-step` serially at the interval `cadence-step` computes.  There is\n9:    no second state owner, and no line handler evaluates, requests, polls,\n10:    or prints.\"\n11:   (:require #?@(:cljd [[\"dart:async\" :as async]\n12:                        [\"dart:convert\" :as convert]\n13:                        [\"dart:core\" :as dart-core]\n14:                        [\"dart:io\" :as io]]\n15:                 :jolt [[dao.host.text :as text]\n16:                        [dao.posix.file :as posix-file]\n17:                        [yang.python.antlr.parser :as python.antlr.parser]\n18:                        [yin.repl.host.common :as host-common]])\n19:             [clojure.string :as str]\n20:             [dao.space.store.fs :as fs]\n21:             [dao.stream.datagram :as datagram]\n22:             [dao.stream.remote-channel :as remote-channel]\n23:             [dao.stream.waitset.cadence :as cadence]\n24:             [dao.stream.waitset.driver :as wake]\n25:             [yin.repl :as shell]\n26:             [yin.repl.connect :as connect]\n27:             [yin.repl.dht :as repl.dht]\n28:             [yin.repl.frontends :as repl.frontends]\n29:             [yin.repl.driver :as driver]\n30:             [yin.repl.host :as host]\n31:             [yin.repl.serve :as serve]\n32:             [yin.repl.state :as state]\n33:             [yin.repl.store :as store]\n34:             [yin.vm.linker.sign :as sign]\n35:             [yin.vm.ucf.compose :as compose]))\n36: \n37: \n38: (def tick-millis\n39:   \"The base cadence of the single step owner.  Slow enough to cost nothing\n40:    while anything moves, fast enough that a remote result prints without\n41:    waiting for a keystroke.\"\n42:   25)\n43: \n44: \n45: (def default-cadence\n46:   \"The tick owner's idle curve.  `tick-millis` is the base interval, what a\n47:    wake resets to and what a pending write holds, and an idle composition\n48:    backs off doubling to 200 ms.  Local input and the explicit stop trigger\n49:    nudge the wake source, so operator actions never wait the curve out; a\n50:    lost nudge costs at most the armed interval.\"\n51:   {:poll-ms tick-millis\n52:    :backoff {:factor 2 :ceiling-ms 200}})\n53: \n54: \n55: (def prompt \"yin> \")\n56: \n57: \n58: (def telemetry-text\n59:   (str \"--telemetry and --telemetry-stream are not part of the DaoStream \"\n60:        \"REPL slice: the telemetry emit path is built (yin.vm.telemetry, opt-in \"\n61:        \"via the :telemetry {:stream ...} construction option (\"\n62:        \"yin.vm.telemetry.implementation-plan.md), but composing a sink into \"\n63:        \"this shell is not, so the flags are rejected rather than ignored\"))\n64: \n65: \n66: (defn- parse-int\n67:   \"The decimal integer `text` names, or nil: total on every host.  At\n68:    most 15 digits, so the value is exact on every host (below 2^53) and\n69:    no host parser can overflow; a longer number is nil, which each flag\n70:    refuses as data.\"\n71:   [text]\n72:   (when (and (string? text) (re-matches #\"-?\\d{1,15}\" text))\n73:     #?(:cljd (int/parse text)\n74:        :cljs (js/parseInt text 10)\n75:        :clj (Long/parseLong text))))\n76: \n77: \n78: (defn parse-peer\n79:   \"A `--dht-peer` value, `host:port` or `[v6-host]:port`, as the DHT's\n80:    bootstrap contact `{:host h :port p}`.  The host must be an IP\n81:    literal: the DHT resolves no names.\"\n82:   [text]\n83:   (let [[_ v6 v4 port] (re-matches #\"(?:\\[([^\\]]+)\\]|([^:\\[\\]]+)):(\\d+)\"\n84:                                    (str text))\n85:         host (or v6 v4)\n86:         port (parse-int port)]\n87:     (cond\n88:       (not (datagram/valid-port? port))\n89:       (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-peer takes host:port with a port from 1 \"\n90:                            \"to 65535, not \" (pr-str text))\n91:                       {:value text}))\n92: \n93:       (not (datagram/ip-literal? host))\n94:       (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-peer host must be an IP literal, not \"\n95:                            (pr-str host))\n96:                       {:value text}))\n97: \n98:       :else {:host host :port port})))\n99: \n100: \n101: (defn- parse-manifest\n102:   \"A `--dht-manifest` value (the address as printed, with or without its\n103:    leading colon) as the segment address keyword.\"\n104:   [text]\n105:   (when (string? text)\n106:     (keyword (if (str/starts-with? text \":\") (subs text 1) text))))\n107: \n108: \n109: (def ^:private dht-flags\n110:   #{\"--dht-peer\" \"--dht-publish\" \"--dht-bind\" \"--dht-port\"\n111:     \"--dht-max-inbound-bytes\" \"--dht-manifest\" \"--dht-key\" \"--dht-principal\"\n112:     \"--dht-trust\" \"--dht-follow\"})\n113: \n114: \n115: (def max-follow\n116:   \"The most principals one node follows (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 7).\"\n117:   64)\n118: \n119: \n120: (defn parse-follow\n121:   \"A `--dht-follow` value, `<64 hex>@<host:port>`, as `{:principal hex\n122:    :host h :port p}`: the principal whose published head is followed, at\n123:    the one address its board is dialed at.  The host must be a loopback\n124:    IP literal (`localhost` is 127.0.0.1): following off loopback is\n125:    not in this release (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.1, 8.3).\"\n126:   [text]\n127:   (let [[_ principal v6 v4 port]\n128:         (re-matches #\"(?:ed25519:)?([0-9a-f]{64})@(?:\\[([^\\]]+)\\]|([^:\\[\\]@]+)):(\\d+)\"\n129:                     (str/replace-first (str text) #\"@localhost:\" \"@127.0.0.1:\"))\n130:         host (or v6 v4)\n131:         port (parse-int port)]\n132:     (cond\n133:       (nil? principal)\n134:       (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-follow takes <64 lowercase hex>@<host:port>,\"\n135:                            \" not \" (pr-str text))\n136:                       {:value text}))\n137: \n138:       (not (datagram/valid-port? port))\n139:       (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-follow takes a port from 1 to 65535, not \"\n140:                            (pr-str text))\n141:                       {:value text}))\n142: \n143:       (not (remote-channel/loopback-literal? host))\n144:       (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-follow \" (pr-str text) \": a head board is \"\n145:                            \"followed on loopback only (127.0.0.1 or ::1)\")\n146:                       {:value text}))\n147: \n148:       :else {:principal principal :host host :port port})))\n149: \n150: \n151: (defn parse-principal\n152:   \"A `--dht-principal` value: a declared publisher's public key, exactly\n153:    64 lowercase hexadecimal characters (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 6.2).\"\n154:   [text]\n155:   (if (and (string? text) (re-matches #\"^[0-9a-f]{64}$\" text))\n156:     text\n157:     (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-principal takes a public key of 64 lowercase \"\n158:                          \"hexadecimal characters, not \" (pr-str text))\n159:                     {:value text}))))\n160: \n161: \n162: (defn parse-trust\n163:   \"Parse the shared CLI/session trust configuration principal.\"\n164:   [text]\n165:   (state/parse-trust text))\n166: \n167: \n168: ;; =============================================================================\n169: ;; The publisher's key file (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 6.5)\n170: ;; =============================================================================\n171: \n172: (defn- split-path\n173:   \"`[dir name]` of a file path.\"\n174:   [path]\n175:   (if-let [i (str/last-index-of path \"/\")]\n176:     [(subs path 0 i) (subs path (inc i))]\n177:     [\".\" path]))\n178: \n179: \n180: (defn load-key\n181:   \"The publisher key `{:seed :public}` the key file at `path` holds, or a\n182:    refusal of startup with the reason: a file that does not exist, or one\n183:    `yin.vm.linker.sign/key-from-text` refuses.  Nothing falls back to a\n184:    fresh key, and no refusal carries the file's content.\"\n185:   [path]\n186:   (let [[dir name] (split-path path)\n187:         text (fs/read-file-text dir name)\n188:         key (when text (sign/key-from-text text))]\n189:     (cond\n190:       (nil? text)\n191:       (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-key \" path \": the key file does not exist; \"\n192:                            \"nothing falls back to a fresh key (create one with \"\n193:                            \"--dht-keygen \" path \")\")\n194:                       {:path path}))\n195: \n196:       (= :refused (:status key))\n197:       (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-key \" path \": the key file is refused (\"\n198:                            (:reason key) \")\")\n199:                       {:path path :reason (:reason key)}))\n200: \n201:       :else key)))\n202: \n203: \n204: (defn- ensure-parent!\n205:   \"Create the missing directories above `path`.\"\n206:   [path]\n207:   (let [[dir _] (split-path path)]\n208:     #?(:cljd (.createSync (io/Directory. dir) .recursive true)\n209:        :clj (.mkdirs (java.io.File. ^String dir))\n210:        :cljs (.mkdirSync (js/require \"fs\") dir #js {:recursive true}))))\n211: \n212: \n213: (defn- write-new-file!\n214:   \"Create `path` holding `text`, readable by its owner only where the host\n215:    can set that, refusing an existing file: a key file is never\n216:    overwritten.  The directories above it are created.\"\n217:   [path text]\n218:   (ensure-parent! path)\n219:   (let [exists (fn []\n220:                  (ex-info (str \"--dht-keygen \" path \": the file exists; \"\n221:                                \"a key file is never overwritten\")\n222:                           {:path path}))]\n223:     #?(:cljd (let [f (io/File. path)]\n224:                (when (.existsSync f) (throw (exists)))\n225:                (try (.createSync f .exclusive true)\n226:                     (catch Object _ (throw (exists))))\n227:                (.writeAsStringSync f text))\n228:        ;; O_CREAT | O_EXCL at 0600 in one call: owner-only from creation,\n229:        ;; never an existing file; written whole, fsynced, closed checked\n230:        :jolt (when (= :exists (posix-file/write-new-private! path (text/utf8-encode text)))\n231:                (throw (exists)))\n232:        :clj (let [p (.toPath (java.io.File. ^String path))\n233:                   owner-only\n234:                   (java.nio.file.attribute.PosixFilePermissions/asFileAttribute\n235:                     (java.nio.file.attribute.PosixFilePermissions/fromString\n236:                       \"rw-------\"))]\n237:               (try\n238:                 (try (java.nio.file.Files/createFile\n239:                        p (into-array java.nio.file.attribute.FileAttribute\n240:                                      [owner-only]))\n241:                      (catch UnsupportedOperationException _\n242:                        (java.nio.file.Files/createFile\n243:                          p (make-array java.nio.file.attribute.FileAttribute\n244:                                        0))))\n245:                 (catch java.nio.file.FileAlreadyExistsException _\n246:                   (throw (exists))))\n247:               (spit path text))\n248:        :cljs (let [fs-module (js/require \"fs\")]\n249:                (try (.writeFileSync fs-module path text #js {:flag \"wx\" :mode\n250:                                                              384})\n251:                     (catch :default e\n252:                       (if (= \"EEXIST\" (.-code e))\n253:                         (throw (exists))\n254:                         (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-keygen \" path \": \"\n255:                                              (.-message e))\n256:                                         {:path path})))))))))\n257: \n258: \n259: (defn keygen!\n260:   \"Write a new key, from the host CSPRNG, to a new key file at `path`\n261:    (`--dht-keygen`), and answer the lines to print and the exit status.\n262:    The seed is never printed.\"\n263:   [path]\n264:   (let [key (sign/generate)]\n265:     (write-new-file! path (str (sign/key-text key) \"\\n\"))\n266:     {:lines (cond-> [(str \"dht: wrote a new Ed25519 key to \" path\n267:                           \"; its principal is \"\n268:                           (sign/principal (:public key))\n269:                           \". Keep the file: a lost key \"\n270:                           \"can never sign again, and a new key is a new \"\n271:                           \"principal.\")]\n272:               ;; dart:io sets no file permissions (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 6.5)\n273:               #?(:cljd true :default false)\n274:               (conj (str \"dht: WARNING: this host cannot restrict the k\"\n275:                          \"ey file's \"\n276:                          \"permissions; it is readable as the process um\"\n277:                          \"ask allows. \"\n278:                          \"Restrict it now: chmod 600 \" path)))\n279:      :exit 0}))\n280: \n281: \n282: (defn- dht-spec\n283:   \"Fold the `--dht-*` flags into the `dht:<dir>` spec, checked by\n284:    `yin.repl.store/checked-spec`.  They mean nothing to any other store,\n285:    and a bind address means nothing to a solo node, so each such use is\n286:    refused rather than ignored.\"\n287:   [spec {:keys [flags peers publish? bind-host bind-port max-inbound-bytes\n288:                 manifest follow principals]}]\n289:   (cond\n290:     (and (seq flags) (not= :dht (:type spec)))\n291:     (throw (ex-info (str (str/join \", \" (sort flags))\n292:                          \" need --index-store dht:<dir>\")\n293:                     {:flags (vec (sort flags))}))\n294: \n295:     (not= :dht (:type spec)) spec\n296: \n297:     (and (empty? peers) (or bind-host bind-port))\n298:     (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-bind and --dht-port need a --dht-peer: \"\n299:                          \"with no peers the DHT store is solo and opens \"\n300:                          \"no socket\")\n301:                     {}))\n302: \n303:     (and bind-host (not (datagram/ip-literal? bind-host)))\n304:     (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-bind must be an IP literal, not \"\n305:                          (pr-str bind-host))\n306:                     {:value bind-host}))\n307: \n308:     (and (seq follow) (empty? peers))\n309:     (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-follow needs a --dht-peer: a followed head's \"\n310:                          \"index is fetched over the DHT\")\n311:                     {}))\n312: \n313:     (some #(not (contains? (set principals) (:principal %))) follow)\n314:     (let [p (:principal (first (remove #(contains? (set principals)\n315:                                                    (:principal %))\n316:                                        follow)))]\n317:       (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-follow \" p \" follows a principal that is not\"\n318:                            \" declared: add --dht-principal \" p)\n319:                       {:principal p})))\n320: \n321:     (not= (count follow) (count (distinct (map :principal follow))))\n322:     (throw (ex-info \"--dht-follow names each principal once: one source each\"\n323:                     {}))\n324: \n325:     (> (count follow) max-follow)\n326:     (throw (ex-info (str \"at most \" max-follow \" principals may be followed\")\n327:                     {:count (count follow)}))\n328: \n329:     :else\n330:     (store/checked-spec\n331:       (cond-> (assoc spec :peers (vec peers) :publish? (boolean publish?))\n332:         bind-host (assoc :bind-host bind-host)\n333:         bind-port (assoc :bind-port bind-port)\n334:         max-inbound-bytes (assoc :max-inbound-bytes max-inbound-bytes)\n335:         manifest (assoc :manifest manifest)\n336:         ;; a pin wins for the run: it follows nothing (5.6)\n337:         (and (seq follow) (not manifest)) (assoc :follow (vec follow))\n338:         (and (seq follow) manifest) (assoc :follow-suspended true)))))\n339: \n340: \n341: (defn- dht-number\n342:   [flag text]\n343:   (or (parse-int text)\n344:       (throw (ex-info (str flag \" takes an integer of at most 15 digits, not \"\n345:                            (pr-str text))\n346:                       {:value text}))))\n347: \n348: \n349: ;; =============================================================================\n350: ;; The subcommands: `keygen` and `dht init|serve|join`\n351: ;; =============================================================================\n352: \n353: (defn- home-dir*\n354:   \"The user's home directory, or nil where the host has none (ClojureDart).\"\n355:   []\n356:   #?(:cljd nil\n357:      :clj (System/getProperty \"user.home\")\n358:      :cljs (some-> js/process .-env .-HOME)))\n359: \n360: \n361: (defn- home-dir\n362:   []\n363:   (or (home-dir*)\n364:       (throw (ex-info (str \"no home directory on this host: give --dir and \"\n365:                            \"--key explicitly\")\n366:                       {}))))\n367: \n368: \n369: (defn- state-path\n370:   \"`~/.yin/<name><suffix>`.\"\n371:   [name suffix]\n372:   (str (home-dir) \"/.yin/\" name suffix))\n373: \n374: \n375: (defn- peer-text\n376:   \"A peer as the DHT takes it: `localhost` is the loopback IP literal.\"\n377:   [text]\n378:   (str/replace-first (str text) #\"^localhost:\" \"127.0.0.1:\"))\n379: \n380: \n381: (defn parse-token\n382:   \"A join token `yin:<host:port>/<principal>` as `[peer principal]` flag\n383:    values; the principal accepts an `ed25519:` prefix.  A token with a\n384:    third part is refused: a manifest is a pin and belongs to\n385:    `--dht-manifest` (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.8).\"\n386:   [text]\n387:   (let [[_ peer principal more]\n388:         (re-matches #\"yin:([^/]+)/(?:ed25519:)?([^/]+)(/.*)?\" (str text))]\n389:     (cond\n390:       (and peer more)\n391:       (throw (ex-info (str \"a join token has two parts, yin:<host:port>/\"\n392:                            \"<principal>; \" (pr-str text) \" has a third: a \"\n393:                            \"manifest is a pin and belongs to --dht-manifest\")\n394:                       {:value text}))\n395: \n396:       (not peer)\n397:       (throw (ex-info (str \"a join token looks like yin:<host:port>/\"\n398:                            \"<principal>, as `dht: join token:` prints it, \"\n399:                            \"not \" (pr-str text))\n400:                       {:value text}))\n401: \n402:       :else [peer principal])))\n403: \n404: \n405: (defn- keygen-args\n406:   [args]\n407:   (loop [args (seq args) name nil file nil]\n408:     (if-let [arg (first args)]\n409:       (if (= \"--name\" arg)\n410:         (recur (nnext args) (second args) file)\n411:         (recur (next args) name arg))\n412:       [\"--dht-keygen\" (or file (state-path (or name \"publisher\") \".key\"))])))\n413: \n414: \n415: (defn- dht-args\n416:   \"The legacy flags of `dht init|serve|join`.  `init` publishes (its key is\n417:    made on first use); `serve` stores for others; `join <token>` reads,\n418:    declaring and following the token's principal at its address.\n419:    Every node needs a `--peer` to open a socket (the two-node start in\n420:    yin.repl.md); `--listen [ip:]port` is the node's own address.  Returns\n421:    `[args {:new-key path}]`.\"\n422:   [[verb & args]]\n423:   (when-not (#{\"init\" \"serve\" \"join\"} verb)\n424:     (throw (ex-info \"usage: yin-repl dht init|serve|join [token] [options]\"\n425:                     {})))\n426:   (loop [args (seq args)\n427:          o {:name \"node\" :peers [] :rest []}]\n428:     (if-let [arg (first args)]\n429:       (let [v (second args)]\n430:         (case arg\n431:           \"--name\" (recur (nnext args) (assoc o :name v))\n432:           \"--dir\" (recur (nnext args) (assoc o :dir v))\n433:           \"--key\" (recur (nnext args) (assoc o :key v))\n434:           \"--peer\" (recur (nnext args) (update o :peers conj (peer-text v)))\n435:           \"--listen\" (recur (nnext args) (assoc o :listen v))\n436:           (if (and (= \"join\" verb) (not (:token o))\n437:                    (not (str/starts-with? arg \"-\")))\n438:             (recur (next args) (assoc o :token arg))\n439:             (recur (next args) (update o :rest conj arg)))))\n440:       (let [{:keys [name dir key peers listen token rest]} o\n441:             [tpeer principal] (when (= \"join\" verb)\n442:                                 (parse-token token))\n443:             tpeer (some-> tpeer peer-text)\n444:             peers (cond-> peers tpeer (conj tpeer))\n445:             [_ lhost lport] (when listen\n446:                               (re-matches #\"(?:(.+):)?(\\d+)\" listen))\n447:             key (or key (when (= \"init\" verb) (state-path name \".key\")))]\n448:         [(cond-> [\"--index-store\" (str \"dht:\" (or dir (state-path name \"\")))]\n449:            (= \"init\" verb) (conj \"--dht-publish\")\n450:            key (into [\"--dht-key\" key])\n451:            lhost (into [\"--dht-bind\" lhost])\n452:            lport (into [\"--dht-port\" lport])\n453:            ;; join follows the principal's head at the token's address and\n454:            ;; passes no manifest: its first manifest is the first head it\n455:            ;; installs (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.8)\n456:            principal (into [\"--dht-principal\" principal\n457:                             \"--dht-follow\" (str principal \"@\" tpeer)])\n458:            true (into (mapcat #(vector \"--dht-peer\" %)) peers)\n459:            true (into rest))\n460:          {:new-key (when (= \"init\" verb) key)\n461:           :name name\n462:           :dir (or dir (state-path name \"\"))\n463:           ;; only `init` publishes and signs by default: a saved --dht-publish\n464:           ;; or --dht-key must not survive `serve` or `join` (explicit flags\n465:           ;; named on that command line still stand)\n466:           :unset (when-not (= \"init\" verb) #{\"--dht-publish\" \"--dht-key\"})}]))))\n467: \n468: \n469: (defn- plain-args\n470:   \"The plain form's `--name n` and `--dir d`, which name the node directory\n471:    its state file lives in (default `~/.yin/node`), removed from `args`.\n472:    Answers `[args extra]`.\"\n473:   [args]\n474:   (loop [args (seq args) rest [] node-name \"node\" dir nil]\n475:     (if-let [arg (first args)]\n476:       (case arg\n477:         \"--name\" (if (next args)\n478:                    (recur (nnext args) rest (second args) dir)\n479:                    (recur (next args) (conj rest arg) node-name dir))\n480:         \"--dir\" (if (next args)\n481:                   (recur (nnext args) rest node-name (second args))\n482:                   (recur (next args) (conj rest arg) node-name dir))\n483:         (recur (next args) (conj rest arg) node-name dir))\n484:       [rest {:name node-name :dir dir}])))\n485: \n486: \n487: (defn expand-args\n488:   \"Turn the subcommand surface into the flags `parse-args` reads:\n489:    `keygen [--name n | file]` and `dht init|serve|join`.  Anything else\n490:    passes through unchanged.  Answers `[args extra]`, `extra` carrying what\n491:    the arguments say beyond flags: `:new-key` (a key file `init` makes),\n492:    `:name` and `:dir` (the node directory), and `:unset` (saved flags the\n493:    subcommand clears).\"\n494:   [args]\n495:   (case (first args)\n496:     \"keygen\" [(keygen-args (rest args)) nil]\n497:     \"dht\" (dht-args (rest args))\n498:     (plain-args args)))\n499: \n500: \n501: (defn- parse-vm\n502:   \"A `--vm` value as the evaluator keyword the shell takes.\"\n503:   [text]\n504:   (let [vm-type (some-> text keyword)]\n505:     (if (contains? shell/vm-labels vm-type)\n506:       vm-type\n507:       (throw (ex-info (str \"--vm takes one of \"\n508:                            (str/join \", \" (map name (sort (keys shell/vm-labels))))\n509:                            \", not \" (pr-str text))\n510:                       {:value text})))))\n511: \n512: \n513: (defn- parse-codec\n514:   \"A `--codec` value, `cbor` or `transit`, as the keyword `connect/codec-profiles`\n515:    names.\"\n516:   [value]\n517:   (let [k (some-> value keyword)]\n518:     (if (contains? connect/codec-profiles k)\n519:       k\n520:       (throw (ex-info (str #?(:cljd \"--codec takes cbor or transit, not \"\n521:                               :jolt \"--codec takes cbor, not \"\n522:                               :default \"--codec takes cbor or transit, not \") (pr-str value))\n523:                       {:value value})))))\n524: \n525: \n526: (defn parse-args\n527:   \"Parse the host arguments.  `--telemetry` and `--telemetry-stream` are\n528:    rejected rather than ignored.  `--index-store` is parsed into\n529:    `:index-store-spec`: `:mem` (also what omission means),\n530:    `{:type :file :dir dir}`, or the DHT store `{:type :dht :dir dir ...}`;\n531:    and a missing value, an unknown scheme, or an empty directory is\n532:    refused here, by `yin.repl.store/parse-arg`, before any host composes.\n533: \n534:    The DHT store's options are their own flags (yin.repl.dht):\n535:    `--dht-peer host:port`, repeatable, the bootstrap contacts; none\n536:    means solo, with no socket; `--dht-publish`, the separate declaration\n537:    that shares the store; `--dht-bind ip` and `--dht-port p`, the\n538:    socket's address, loopback and ephemeral unless given;\n539:    `--dht-max-inbound-bytes n`, the inbound storage bound; and\n540:    `--dht-manifest address`, a remote index to hydrate before the first\n541:    evaluation; `--dht-key file`, the publisher's stable key file\n542:    (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 6.5); `--dht-principal hex`, repeatable, a\n543:    publisher whose signed names this node honors (7.1);\n544:    `--dht-trust ed25519:hex`, repeatable, a principal whose modules the\n545:    session's admission interpreter admits (7.5), apart from the\n546:    declarations; and\n547:    `--dht-follow hex@host:port`, repeatable, a declared publisher whose\n548:    published head this node follows at that loopback address\n549:    (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.5, 5.8).  Any of them\n550:    without `dht:<dir>` is refused.  `--dht-keygen file` writes a new key\n551:    file and exits.\"\n552:   [args]\n553:   (loop [args (seq args)\n554:          opts {:headless? false :host nil :port nil :rejected []\n555:                :index-store-spec :mem}\n556:          dht {:flags #{} :peers []}]\n557:     (if-let [arg (first args)]\n558:       (let [dht (cond-> dht (dht-flags arg) (update :flags conj arg))\n559:             value (second args)]\n560:         (case arg\n561:           \"--port\" (recur (nnext args)\n562:                           (assoc opts :port (when-some [p (second args)]\n563:                                               (let [n (parse-int p)]\n564:                                                 (if (and n (<= 0 n 65535))\n565:                                                   n\n566:                                                   (throw (ex-info\n567:                                                            (str \"--port takes a \"\n568:                                                                 \"port from 0 to \"\n569:                                                                 \"65535, not \"\n570:                                                                 (pr-str p))\n571:                                                            {:value p}))))))\n572:                           dht)\n573:           \"--host\" (throw (ex-info (str \"--host is gone: --port serves on all \"\n574:                                         \"interfaces, so it answers on \"\n575:                                         \"localhost and on this machine's IP\")\n576:                                    {}))\n577:           \"--codec\" (recur (nnext args)\n578:                            (assoc opts :codec (parse-codec value))\n579:                            dht)\n580:           \"--headless\" (recur (next args) (assoc opts :headless? true) dht)\n581:           \"--vm\" (recur (nnext args)\n582:                         (assoc opts :vm-type (parse-vm (second args)))\n583:                         dht)\n584:           \"--index-store\" (recur (nnext args)\n585:                                  (assoc opts\n586:                                         :index-store-spec\n587:                                         (store/parse-arg (second args)))\n588:                                  dht)\n589:           \"--dht-peer\" (recur (nnext args) opts\n590:                               (update dht :peers conj (parse-peer value)))\n591:           \"--dht-publish\" (recur (next args) opts (assoc dht :publish? true))\n592:           \"--dht-bind\" (recur (nnext args) opts\n593:                               (assoc dht :bind-host (str value)))\n594:           \"--dht-port\" (recur (nnext args) opts\n595:                               (assoc dht :bind-port (dht-number arg value)))\n596:           \"--dht-max-inbound-bytes\" (recur (nnext args) opts\n597:                                            (assoc dht :max-inbound-bytes\n598:                                                   (dht-number arg value)))\n599:           \"--dht-manifest\" (recur (nnext args) opts\n600:                                   (assoc dht :manifest (parse-manifest value)))\n601:           \"--dht-key\" (recur (nnext args)\n602:                              (assoc opts :dht-key-file\n603:                                     (or value\n604:                                         (throw (ex-info\n605:                                                  \"--dht-key needs a key file\"\n606:                                                  {}))))\n607:                              dht)\n608:           \"--dht-principal\" (recur (nnext args)\n609:                                    (update opts :principals (fnil conj [])\n610:                                            (parse-principal value))\n611:                                    dht)\n612:           \"--dht-trust\" (recur (nnext args)\n613:                                (update-in opts [:admission :trusted]\n614:                                           (fnil conj #{}) (parse-trust value))\n615:                                dht)\n616:           \"--dht-follow\" (recur (nnext args) opts\n617:                                 (update dht :follow (fnil conj [])\n618:                                         (parse-follow value)))\n619:           \"--dht-keygen\" (recur (nnext args)\n620:                                 (assoc opts :dht-keygen\n621:                                        (or value\n622:                                            (throw (ex-info\n623:                                                     \"--dht-keygen needs a file\"\n624:                                                     {}))))\n625:                                 dht)\n626:           \"--telemetry\" (recur (next args) (update opts :rejected conj arg) dht)\n627:           \"--telemetry-stream\" (recur (nnext args)\n628:                                       (update opts :rejected conj arg) dht)\n629:           (recur (next args) (update opts :extra (fnil conj []) arg) dht)))\n630:       (update opts :index-store-spec dht-spec\n631:               (assoc dht :principals (:principals opts))))))\n632: \n633: \n634: (defn boot\n635:   \"Create the composition: one shell, one input medium, one cursor held only by\n636:    the step owner, and the host WebSocket adapter `(connect ...)` attaches\n637:    through.  The parsed `:index-store-spec` reaches the shell's store\n638:    selection, which resolves and opens it once at construction.  A custody\n639:    composition (:custody, yin.vm.ucf.compose) rides the shell as data; the\n640:    step owner below is the only thing that steps it.\n641: \n642:    `:admission` is the session's admission policy (yin.vm.linker.dht.md\n643:    7.5; `--dht-trust` sets its `:trusted`), by default `{}`: the key's\n644:    own principal only.  It is apart from `:principals`: a declared\n645:    publisher is not a trusted one.\"\n646:   ([] (boot {}))\n647:   ([opts] (driver/create-state\n648:             {:host (or (:adapter opts) (host/websocket))\n649:              :repl (repl.frontends/create-state\n650:                      (cond-> {:index-store-spec (:index-store-spec opts)\n651:                               :dht-key (:dht-key opts)\n652:                               :principals (:principals opts)\n653:                               :admission (:admission opts {})\n654:                               :ws-host (:ws-host opts)\n655:                               :write-heads! (:write-heads! opts)\n656:                               :custody (:custody opts)}\n657:                        (:vm-type opts) (assoc :vm-type (:vm-type opts))))})))\n658: \n659: \n660: (def bind-all-host\n661:   \"`--port` listens on every interface: loopback and the machine's own IP.\"\n662:   \"0.0.0.0\")\n663: \n664: \n665: (defn pick-ip\n666:   \"The address to advertise among a machine's non-loopback IPv4 addresses:\n667:    the first private-network one (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16), since a\n668:    tunnel or VPN interface can list ahead of the LAN's; else the first;\n669:    else loopback.\"\n670:   [ips]\n671:   (or (first (filter #(re-matches #\"10\\..*|192\\.168\\..*|172\\.(1[6-9]|2\\d|3[01])\\..*\"\n672:                                   %)\n673:                      ips))\n674:       (first ips)\n675:       \"127.0.0.1\"))\n676: \n677: \n678: (defn local-ip\n679:   \"This machine's address for `--port`'s banner and advertised host.\n680:    ClojureDart can only list interfaces asynchronously, so it advertises\n681:    loopback.\"\n682:   []\n683:   (pick-ip\n684:     #?(:cljd nil\n685:        :clj (try (doall\n686:                    (for [^java.net.NetworkInterface ni\n687:                          (enumeration-seq\n688:                            (java.net.NetworkInterface/getNetworkInterfaces))\n689:                          :when (and (.isUp ni) (not (.isLoopback ni)))\n690:                          ^java.net.InetAddress a (enumeration-seq\n691:                                                    (.getInetAddresses ni))\n692:                          :when (instance? java.net.Inet4Address a)]\n693:                      (.getHostAddress a)))\n694:                  (catch Exception _ nil))\n695:        :cljs (try (doall\n696:                     (for [addrs (array-seq (js/Object.values\n697:                                              (.networkInterfaces\n698:                                                (js/require \"os\"))))\n699:                           ^js a (array-seq addrs)\n700:                           :when (and (contains? #{\"IPv4\" 4} (.-family a))\n701:                                      (not (.-internal a)))]\n702:                       (.-address a)))\n703:                   (catch :default _ nil)))))\n704: \n705: \n706: (defn boot-server\n707:   \"Compose the served endpoint for `--port`, or nil when no port was asked for.\n708: \n709:    `serve!` returns immediately.  Whether a listener bound is a fact the\n710:    endpoint's lifecycle medium reports, and the ticker prints it.\"\n711:   [opts]\n712:   (when (:port opts)\n713:     (serve/serve! {:bind-port (:port opts)\n714:                    :bind-host bind-all-host\n715:                    :advertised-host (local-ip)\n716:                    :host (or (:adapter opts) (host/websocket))\n717:                    :codec (get connect/codec-profiles (:codec opts :cbor))})))\n718: \n719: \n720: (def help-lines\n721:   \"What `--help` prints: every subcommand and flag `parse-args` and\n722:    `expand-args` read.\"\n723:   [\"yin-repl: the interface to datom.world\"\n724:    \"\"\n725:    \"usage: yin-repl [flags]\"\n726:    \"       yin-repl dht init|serve|join [token] [options]\"\n727:    \"       yin-repl keygen [--name n | file]\"\n728:    \"\"\n729:    \"subcommands:\"\n730:    \"  dht init          publish: share the store, make the key on first run\"\n731:    \"  dht serve         storing peer: fetch only, no key\"\n732:    \"  dht join <token>  reader: follow the head of the token's principal;\"\n733:    \"                    the token is yin:<host:port>/<principal>\"\n734:    \"  keygen            write a new Ed25519 key file and exit\"\n735:    \"\"\n736:    \"dht options:\"\n737:    \"  --name n          state under ~/.yin/<n> (default: node)\"\n738:    \"  --dir d           state directory, instead of ~/.yin/<name>\"\n739:    \"  --key file        key file (init: default ~/.yin/<name>.key)\"\n740:    \"  --listen [ip:]port  this node's DHT socket\"\n741:    \"  --peer host:port  a peer to contact; repeatable; localhost or an IP\"\n742:    \"\"\n743:    \"flags:\"\n744:    \"  --port n          serve this shell to other shells over WebSockets, on\"\n745:    \"                    all interfaces: localhost and this machine's IP\"\n746:    \"  --codec c         wire codec served: cbor (default) or transit\"\n747:    \"  --headless        no prompt, endpoint only; needs --port\"\n748:    \"  --index-store s   mem (default), file:<dir> or dht:<dir>\"\n749:    \"  --vm type         ast-walker, semantic (default), stack or register\"\n750:    \"  --name n, --dir d the node directory holding the saved state\"\n751:    \"                    (default ~/.yin/node)\"\n752:    \"  --reset           forget the saved state; start from the command line\"\n753:    \"  --no-state        neither read nor write the saved state\"\n754:    \"  --help, -h        print this and exit\"\n755:    \"\"\n756:    \"saved state: the flags a node starts with are kept in <node dir>/state.edn,\"\n757:    \"and a bare `yin-repl` starts that node again. Flags change it: a repeated\"\n758:    \"flag (--dht-peer, --dht-principal, --dht-trust, --dht-follow) replaces\"\n759:    \"its saved values, and the result is saved. --dht-manifest and\"\n760:    \"--dht-keygen are never saved. A followed head is kept in\"\n761:    \"<dht dir>/heads.edn.\"\n762:    \"\"\n763:    \"dht flags (need --index-store dht:<dir>, except --dht-keygen):\"\n764:    \"  --dht-peer host:port        bootstrap contact (IP literal); repeatable\"\n765:    \"  --dht-publish               share the store's content with peers\"\n766:    \"  --dht-bind ip               socket address; needs a peer\"\n767:    \"  --dht-port p                socket port; needs a peer\"\n768:    \"  --dht-max-inbound-bytes n   inbound payload bound (default 64 MiB)\"\n769:    \"  --dht-manifest :segment/... remote index to hydrate first; needs a peer;\"\n770:    \"                              a pin: this run follows nothing\"\n771:    \"  --dht-key file              the publisher's key file\"\n772:    \"  --dht-principal hex         a publisher whose signed names to honor;\"\n773:    \"                              repeatable\"\n774:    \"  --dht-trust ed25519:hex     a principal whose modules to admit (not\"\n775:    \"                              implied by --dht-principal); repeatable\"\n776:    \"  (trust p), (untrust p), (trusted) edit/show session trust; reset\"\n777:    \"                              restores the configured --dht-trust set\"\n778:    \"  --dht-follow hex@host:port  follow a declared publisher's head at its\"\n779:    \"                              loopback address; repeatable\"\n780:    \"  --dht-keygen file           write a new key file and exit\"\n781:    \"\"\n782:    \"--telemetry and --telemetry-stream are rejected.\"\n783:    \"Unrecognized arguments are ignored.\"\n784:    \"See src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md for the walk-through.\"])\n785: \n786: \n787: (defn- save-state!\n788:   \"Save the flags this run resolved to the node directory's state file when\n789:    they differ from what was saved (nothing is written for a bare run with\n790:    nothing saved), and answer the banner's lines about it.  A file that\n791:    cannot be written is a warning, never a refusal: the node still starts.\"\n792:   [dir saved resolved reset?]\n793:   (let [path (state/path dir)\n794:         changed (state/changed (or saved {}) resolved)]\n795:     (try\n796:       (cond\n797:         (and (nil? saved) (empty? resolved) (not reset?))\n798:         []\n799: \n800:         (nil? saved)\n801:         (do (state/save! dir resolved)\n802:             [(str \"state: \" (if reset? \"reset, \" \"\") \"saved to \" path\n803:                   \"; a bare yin-repl starts this node again\")])\n804: \n805:         (seq changed)\n806:         (do (state/save! dir resolved)\n807:             [(str \"state: resumed from \" path \" (\" (state/describe saved)\n808:                   \"); the command line changed \" (str/join \", \" changed)\n809:                   \"; saved\")])\n810: \n811:         :else\n812:         [(str \"state: resumed from \" path \" (\" (state/describe resolved) \")\")])\n813:       (catch #?(:cljd Object\n814:                 :clj Throwable\n815:                 :cljs :default)\n816:              e\n817:         [(str \"state: WARNING: could not save \" path \": \"\n818:               (or (ex-message e) (str e)))]))))\n819: \n820: \n821: (defn startup\n822:   \"Parse the arguments and compose the whole shell (the store the parsed\n823:    `:index-store-spec` names included), or answer the refusal text.  This\n824:    is the one gate every host's `-main` passes through before it prints a\n825:    banner or starts a loop, so an invalid `--index-store`, an unsupported\n826:    host for it, or a directory that cannot be opened refuses startup with\n827:    its reason rather than composing a shell around a fallback store.\n828: \n829:    Only a designed refusal is answered: an error carrying no ex-info\n830:    keeps its stack trace, because it is a defect, not an operator error.\n831: \n832:    `--dht-keygen` composes nothing: it writes the key file and answers\n833:    `{:lines [...] :exit 0}` for the host to print and exit with.  A\n834:    `--dht-key` file is loaded here (`load-key`); the key reaches the\n835:    shell, and only its principal reaches the options the banner reads.\n836:    An `:adapter` option is the host WebSocket adapter the shell, the head\n837:    board and the endpoint compose over (else `host/websocket`).\"\n838:   ([args] (startup args nil))\n839:   ([args {:keys [persist? home adapter]}]\n840:    (try\n841:      (if (some #{\"--help\" \"-h\"} args)\n842:        {:lines help-lines :exit 0}\n843:        (let [reset? (boolean (some #{\"--reset\"} args))\n844:              no-state? (boolean (some #{\"--no-state\"} args))\n845:              [args {:keys [new-key unset] node-name :name dir :dir}]\n846:              (expand-args (remove #{\"--reset\" \"--no-state\"} args))\n847:              node-dir (when (and persist? (not no-state?)\n848:                                  (not (some #{\"--dht-keygen\"} args)))\n849:                         (or dir\n850:                             (some-> (or home (home-dir*))\n851:                                     (str \"/.yin/\" node-name))))\n852:              saved (when (and node-dir (not reset?)) (state/load-flags node-dir))\n853:              {:keys [flags rest]} (state/split-args args)\n854:              resolved (state/resolve-flags saved flags unset)\n855:              args (into (state/flags->args resolved) rest)\n856:              made (when (and new-key (nil? (fs/read-file-text\n857:                                              (first (split-path new-key))\n858:                                              (second (split-path new-key)))))\n859:                     (:lines (keygen! new-key)))\n860:              opts (cond-> (try (parse-args args)\n861:                                (catch #?(:cljd Object\n862:                                          :clj Throwable\n863:                                          :cljs :default)\n864:                                       e\n865:                                  (throw (if (and saved (ex-data e))\n866:                                           (ex-info (str (ex-message e)\n867:                                                         \" (with the saved state in \"\n868:                                                         (state/path node-dir)\n869:                                                         \"; --reset forgets it)\")\n870:                                                    (ex-data e))\n871:                                           e))))\n872:                     made (assoc :startup-lines made)\n873:                     adapter (assoc :adapter adapter))]\n874:          (if-some [path (:dht-keygen opts)]\n875:            (keygen! path)\n876:            (let [key (some-> (:dht-key-file opts) load-key)\n877:                  opts (cond-> opts\n878:                         key (assoc :publisher (sign/principal (:public key))))\n879:                  ;; the head board is served and dialed over the host's\n880:                  ;; WebSocket seam (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.1)\n881:                  state' (boot (assoc opts\n882:                                      :dht-key key\n883:                                      :ws-host (or (:adapter opts)\n884:                                                   (host/websocket))))\n885:                  server (boot-server opts)\n886:                  lines (into (vec (repl.dht/follow-lines (:repl state')))\n887:                              (when node-dir\n888:                                (save-state! node-dir saved resolved reset?)))]\n889:              {:opts (cond-> opts\n890:                       (seq lines) (update :startup-lines\n891:                                           #(into (vec %) lines)))\n892:               :state state'\n893:               :server server}))))\n894:      (catch #?(:cljd Object\n895:                :clj Throwable\n896:                :cljs :default)\n897:             e\n898:        (if (ex-data e)\n899:          {:refusal (or (ex-message e) (str e))}\n900:          (throw e))))))\n901: \n902: \n903: (defn- refuse!\n904:   \"Print why the composition refused to start and end the process with a\n905:    failing status, so a refused startup never looks like a working shell.\"\n906:   [refusal]\n907:   (println refusal)\n908:   #?(:cljd (io/exit 1)\n909:      :jolt (do (flush) (System/exit 1))\n910:      :clj (.halt (Runtime/getRuntime) 1)\n911:      :cljs (.exit js/process 1)))\n912: \n913: \n914: (defn- custody-of\n915:   [state]\n916:   (get-in state [:repl :custody]))\n917: \n918: \n919: (defn close-index-store!\n920:   \"Release the composition's lifecycle resources before the host exits:\n921:    the custody composition's journals first (compose/close! -- the\n922:    authority and every journal backend the composition opened; it never\n923:    throws, so a failed close never blocks the exit), then the index\n924:    store's own resources (in durable mode, the exclusive directory\n925:    lock).  The shell has already stopped when a host calls this; the\n926:    memory store has nothing to release.  A DHT store's head board and\n927:    dials have been asked to stop and drained by then (`stop-tick`);\n928:    `yin.repl.dht/close!` is the last resort for whatever the budget\n929:    left.\"\n930:   [state]\n931:   (when-some [c (custody-of state)]\n932:     (compose/close! c))\n933:   (repl.dht/close! (:repl state))\n934:   (store/close! (get-in state [:repl :index-store])))\n935: \n936: \n937: (defn- entry-text\n938:   [entry]\n939:   (or (get entry driver/text-key) (get entry serve/text-key)))\n940: \n941: \n942: (defn banner\n943:   \"Text the composition prints before the first prompt, given parsed\n944:    options.  A DHT store states here (before its node steps once, so\n945:    before anything is shared) what it will share: the whole store when\n946:    `--dht-publish` is given, nothing otherwise (yin.repl.dht/banner).\"\n947:   [opts]\n948:   (let [spec (:index-store-spec opts)\n949:         dht? (= :dht (:type spec))]\n950:     (cond-> (vec (:startup-lines opts))\n951:       (seq (:rejected opts)) (conj telemetry-text)\n952:       (and (:port opts) (zero? (:port opts)))\n953:       (conj (str \"serving on all interfaces on an ephemeral port; the Serving\"\n954:                  \" line names it once bound. Anyone who can reach this port\"\n955:                  \" can evaluate code in this shell; there is no\"\n956:                  \" authentication.\"))\n957: \n958:       (and (:port opts) (not (zero? (:port opts))))\n959:       (conj (str \"serving on all interfaces: ws://127.0.0.1:\" (:port opts)\n960:                  (let [ip (local-ip)]\n961:                    (if (= \"127.0.0.1\" ip)\n962:                      \" and ws://<this machine's IP>:\"\n963:                      (str \" and ws://\" ip \":\")))\n964:                  (:port opts)\n965:                  \". Anyone who can reach this port can evaluate code in this\"\n966:                  \" shell; there is no authentication.\"))\n967:       (and (:headless? opts) (not (:port opts)))\n968:       (conj \"--headless has nothing to attend without a served endpoint\")\n969:       dht? (into (repl.dht/banner spec))\n970:       (and dht? (:publish? spec))\n971:       (conj (str \"dht: the code index and the code itself are shared: every\"\n972:                  \" evaluated program's rows are written to the index store\"\n973:                  \" each round\"))\n974:       (and dht? (:publisher opts))\n975:       (conj (str \"dht: names published here are signed by principal \"\n976:                  (:publisher opts) \" (key from \" (:dht-key-file opts) \")\"))\n977:       (and dht? (not (:publisher opts)))\n978:       (conj (str \"dht: no --dht-key: (yin.link/publish ...) is refused; names\"\n979:                  \" are still read, resolved, loaded and linked\"))\n980:       #?(:cljd false :jolt true :default false)\n981:       (into [shell/host-text\n982:              ;; J5: the native parser is disclosed; with no usable library\n983:              ;; Python is left out of the catalog and the reason is printed\n984:              #?(:jolt (if (python.antlr.parser/available?)\n985:                         (str \"python: the native C++ ANTLR4 parser\"\n986:                              \" (native/jolt-parser), in process; no process\"\n987:                              \" crash isolation\")\n988:                         \"python: unavailable-parser (the native parser library is missing or incompatible; bb build:jolt-native)\")\n989:                 :default nil)]))))\n990: \n991: \n992: (defn- exit-with!\n993:   \"Print a composition that ends at startup -- `--dht-keygen` -- and end\n994:    the process with its status.\"\n995:   [{:keys [lines exit]}]\n996:   (doseq [line lines]\n997:     (println line))\n998:   #?(:cljd (io/exit exit)\n999:      :jolt (do (flush) (System/exit exit))\n1000:      :clj (.halt (Runtime/getRuntime) (int exit))\n1001:      :cljs (.exit js/process exit)))\n1002: \n1003: \n1004: (defn- custody-control-step\n1005:   \"The custody control plane's tick (r3 1.12): the renewals, pending\n1006:    releases and request retries of every holder, the judge step and the\n1007:    front steps of whatever custody composition the shell carries.  It\n1008:    runs immediately after the DHT step and before the refusal and\n1009:    admission branches, so custody progress and cleanup do not wait for\n1010:    a hydration to finish and survive a refused store.  No program work\n1011:    happens here whatever the shell's state.\"\n1012:   [state]\n1013:   (if-some [c (custody-of state)]\n1014:     (assoc-in state [:repl :custody] (compose/control-step c))\n1015:     state))\n1016: \n1017: \n1018: (defn- custody-program-step\n1019:   \"The custody program step's tick: activation, execution and export\n1020:    preparation under the split driver's own revalidated tenure.  It runs\n1021:    after the shell's own step and before the endpoint's, and never once\n1022:    the shell has stopped -- the stop latch is set in the shell's place.\"\n1023:   [state]\n1024:   (if-some [c (custody-of state)]\n1025:     (assoc-in state [:repl :custody] (compose/program-step c))\n1026:     state))\n1027: \n1028: \n1029: (defn- custody-stop\n1030:   \"Latch the local stop on every holder of the shell's custody\n1031:    composition (compose/stop): program work ends, control cleanup\n1032:    continues through the bounded shutdown drain.  Idempotent, and a\n1033:    no-op with no composition.\"\n1034:   [state]\n1035:   (if-some [c (custody-of state)]\n1036:     (assoc-in state [:repl :custody] (compose/stop c))\n1037:     state))\n1038: \n1039: \n1040: (defn- custody-owed?\n1041:   \"True while the shell's custody composition owes a control-plane\n1042:    write (compose/owed-control-write?): a pending write keeps the tick\n1043:    owner's cadence at the base interval, like the driver's own.\"\n1044:   [state]\n1045:   (if-some [c (custody-of state)]\n1046:     (compose/owed-control-write? c)\n1047:     false))\n1048: \n1049: \n1050: (defn step-all\n1051:   \"One tick of the single step owner: the local shell first, then the served\n1052:    endpoint, against the same shell value.  A DHT index store's node is stepped\n1053:    first (yin.repl.dht/step): its lines print before the shell's, a\n1054:    hydration still outstanding leaves every typed line waiting in the\n1055:    input medium, and a refused one stops the shell.  The custody control\n1056:    plane is stepped next, before the refusal and admission branches, so\n1057:    renewals, pending releases and request retries continue during\n1058:    hydration and past a refusal.  A `--port` process\n1059:    serves one shared shell, as v1's atom made it: the driver evaluates this\n1060:    tick's local\n1061:    lines first, so a definition typed at the local prompt is already in the\n1062:    shell the endpoint evaluates remote requests against in the same tick, and\n1063:    the endpoint's shell (remote definitions included) is threaded back\n1064:    before the next tick.  The custody program step follows the driver's own\n1065:    step and precedes the endpoint's; once the shell has stopped it is\n1066:    replaced by the stop latch, so no custody program call runs after\n1067:    `:running?` turns false.  A require parked on a closure load the node\n1068:    ended this tick is re-checked once, before any typed line, with no line\n1069:    of its own (yin.repl/recheck-on-tick, yin.vm.linker.dht.md 8.2),\n1070:    and linker progress resumes a parked run (7.5): its text prints after the\n1071:    node's lines.  Returns `[state server lines]`;\n1072:    the caller only prints.\"\n1073:   [state server now]\n1074:   (let [[repl dht-lines events] (repl.dht/step (:repl state) now)\n1075:         state (custody-control-step (assoc state :repl repl))]\n1076:     (cond\n1077:       (repl.dht/refusal repl)\n1078:       [(custody-stop (assoc state :running? false)) server dht-lines]\n1079: \n1080:       (not (repl.dht/admitting? repl))\n1081:       [state server dht-lines]\n1082: \n1083:       :else\n1084:       (let [[repl rechecked] (shell/recheck-on-tick (:repl state) events)\n1085:             dht-lines (cond-> dht-lines\n1086:                         (seq rechecked) (conj rechecked))\n1087:             state (assoc state :repl repl)\n1088:             stepped (driver/repl-step state now)\n1089:             stepped (if (:running? stepped)\n1090:                       (custody-program-step stepped)\n1091:                       (custody-stop stepped))\n1092:             [entries state'] (driver/take-outbox stepped)\n1093:             server' (when server\n1094:                       (serve/step (assoc-in server [:repl] (:repl state'))\n1095:                                   now))\n1096:             [server-entries server''] (if server'\n1097:                                         (serve/take-outbox server')\n1098:                                         [[] nil])\n1099:             state'' (if server'' (assoc state' :repl (:repl server'')) state')]\n1100:         [state'' server'' (into dht-lines\n1101:                                 (map entry-text)\n1102:                                 (into (vec entries) server-entries))]))))\n1103: \n1104: \n1105: (defn moved?\n1106:   \"The tick owner's cadence bit, computed from this tick's own results: true\n1107:    when there are lines to print, when the endpoint reports movement (a\n1108:    woken probe, a published notice), or when either composition still owes a\n1109:    write -- the shell's own or a custody control-plane one.  A pending write\n1110:    keeps cadence at the base interval; it must never wait out a backoff\n1111:    ceiling.\"\n1112:   [state server lines]\n1113:   (boolean (or (seq lines)\n1114:                (driver/pending-write? state)\n1115:                (repl.dht/busy? (:repl state))\n1116:                (custody-owed? state)\n1117:                (and server (serve/moved? server)))))\n1118: \n1119: \n1120: (defn prompt-owed?\n1121:   \"True when the operator is owed a fresh prompt after a tick: the tick\n1122:    printed lines, or it consumed input that printed nothing (a silent\n1123:    assignment), which only the input cursor's advance reveals.\"\n1124:   [state state' lines]\n1125:   (boolean (or (seq lines)\n1126:                (not= (:input-cursor state) (:input-cursor state')))))\n1127: \n1128: \n1129: (defn exit-status\n1130:   \"The process status a host exits with once the shell stops: 1 when the\n1131:    DHT store refused after startup (a failed bind, a hydration that could\n1132:    not complete), else 0.\"\n1133:   [state]\n1134:   (if (repl.dht/refusal (:repl state)) 1 0))\n1135: \n1136: \n1137: ;; =============================================================================\n1138: ;; Shutdown\n1139: ;; =============================================================================\n1140: \n1141: (def stop-ticks\n1142:   \"How many ticks a host steps a stopping endpoint before exiting anyway; each\n1143:    tick lasts at least `tick-millis`.  The\n1144:    endpoint's `:stopped` fact comes from the host's own close completion, which\n1145:    a wedged listener may never deposit; the shell says so and exits rather than\n1146:    hanging on it.\"\n1147:   200)\n1148: \n1149: \n1150: (def stop-timeout-text\n1151:   \";; the endpoint did not report :stopped; exiting anyway\")\n1152: \n1153: \n1154: (def stop-join-millis\n1155:   \"How long a host thread may wait for the step owner to finish shutdown.\n1156: \n1157:    The drain performs `stop-ticks + 1` steps with a sleep between them, so it\n1158:    needs strictly more than `tick-millis x stop-ticks`; a join of exactly that\n1159:    expires mid-drain and the announced timeout never prints.\"\n1160:   (* tick-millis (+ stop-ticks 2)))\n1161: \n1162: \n1163: (defn request-stop!\n1164:   \"The explicit stop trigger: append the shell's own quit line to the input\n1165:    medium.\n1166: \n1167:    This is the whole of what a host signal handler, a headless supervisor, or a\n1168:    test does to stop the composition.  It is a line producer like any other, so\n1169:    the one step owner still performs the shutdown: `(quit)` stops the shell,\n1170:    and the shell stopping is what stops the endpoint.  The wake source, when\n1171:    one is supplied, is nudged so the step owner runs the quit line at once\n1172:    rather than at the idle interval it had armed.\"\n1173:   ([state]\n1174:    (driver/submit-line! (:input state) \"(quit)\"))\n1175:   ([state w]\n1176:    (let [result (request-stop! state)]\n1177:      (when w (wake/nudge! w))\n1178:      result)))\n1179: \n1180: \n1181: (defn stop-tick\n1182:   \"One shutdown tick for the shell's head board and the endpoint, both of\n1183:    which have already been asked to stop (`yin.repl.dht/stop!`,\n1184:    `serve/stop!`).  Returns `[state server lines stopped?]`, the node's\n1185:    lines before the endpoint's.\n1186: \n1187:    `serve/stopped?` is the whole of the endpoint's exit condition, and\n1188:    `yin.repl.dht/stopped?` for the shell's head board: an endpoint that never\n1189:    bound is done on the first tick, because no host exists to report a\n1190:    `:stopped` fact for it, an absent endpoint -- a shell run without\n1191:    `--port` -- never had one, and so is a shell with no board.  The custody\n1192:    composition is not stepped here: `shutdown-tick` is the drain tick that\n1193:    wraps this one with the custody control plane.\"\n1194:   [state server now]\n1195:   (let [[repl dht-lines _] (repl.dht/step (:repl state) now)\n1196:         state (assoc state :repl repl)\n1197:         [server' entries] (if server\n1198:                             (let [[entries server'] (serve/take-outbox\n1199:                                                       (serve/step server now))]\n1200:                               [server' entries])\n1201:                             [nil []])]\n1202:     [state server'\n1203:      (into (vec dht-lines) (map entry-text) entries)\n1204:      (and (or (nil? server') (serve/stopped? server'))\n1205:           (repl.dht/stopped? repl))]))\n1206: \n1207: \n1208: (defn shutdown-enter?\n1209:   \"Whether a shell that just stopped enters the bounded shutdown drain at\n1210:    all (r3 1.12 as amended by residual 4 and the D15 gate's shutdown row):\n1211:    there is an endpoint to stop, a head board that still owes its\n1212:    stop-and-drain, or a custody composition that still owes a\n1213:    control-plane write.  A REPL run without --port enters the drain on its\n1214:    owed custody writes alone -- a release owed at quit is sent, never\n1215:    dropped by the process exit.\"\n1216:   [state server]\n1217:   (boolean (or server\n1218:                (get-in state [:repl :dht])\n1219:                (custody-owed? state))))\n1220: \n1221: \n1222: (defn shutdown-drained?\n1223:   \"The bounded shutdown drain's exit condition, the one every host loop\n1224:    tests: the endpoint has stopped -- an absent endpoint owes nothing --\n1225:    AND the shell's head board owes nothing at exit, AND the custody\n1226:    composition owes no further control-plane write.  A pending release,\n1227:    renewal or undelivered reply still owed keeps the drain stepping\n1228:    within its `stop-ticks` budget; the budget, not this predicate, is\n1229:    what bounds a write that can never complete.\"\n1230:   [state server]\n1231:   (boolean (and (serve/stopped? server)\n1232:                 (repl.dht/stopped? (:repl state))\n1233:                 (not (custody-owed? state)))))\n1234: \n1235: \n1236: (defn shutdown-tick\n1237:   \"One tick of the bounded shutdown drain (r3 1.12, residual 4), the tick\n1238:    every host's drain loop calls: the custody control plane keeps running --\n1239:    the holders' local stop latch is set here, at drain entry, on every host,\n1240:    including paths that reached the drain without another normal `step-all`\n1241:    -- and the head board and the endpoint drain (an absent endpoint drains\n1242:    nothing and answers stopped).  No custody program step runs: program\n1243:    execution stays stopped once the shell has.  Returns\n1244:    `[state server lines stopped?]`; the budget (`stop-ticks`) and the exit\n1245:    condition (`shutdown-enter?`, `shutdown-drained?`) are the caller's.\"\n1246:   [state server now]\n1247:   (let [state (custody-control-step (custody-stop state))]\n1248:     (stop-tick state server now)))\n1249: \n1250: \n1251: ;; =============================================================================\n1252: ;; clj: a reader thread that only appends, and one owned poller thread\n1253: ;; =============================================================================\n1254: \n1255: (defn- drain-owner!\n1256:   \"The shell has quit, so the head board, the endpoint and the custody\n1257:         control plane stop before the host exits: ask the board and the\n1258:         endpoint once, then keep stepping all three until the endpoint has\n1259:         stopped AND the board owes nothing AND custody owes no further\n1260:         control write (`shutdown-drained?`), or the bounded budget runs\n1261:         out.  The drain is entered at all only when one of them owes\n1262:         something (`shutdown-enter?` -- a shell with no endpoint enters it\n1263:         on owed custody writes alone).  A connected client must observe the\n1264:         ended answer, not the bare close a process exit would leave behind.\n1265:         Each drain round lasts at least the base interval of wall time,\n1266:         however many early wakes readiness causes; the budget counts rounds,\n1267:         so the drain is at least `tick-millis x stop-ticks` of wall time.\n1268:         The custody control plane steps on every one of its ticks\n1269:         (`shutdown-tick`); the state it reaches is durable in the progress\n1270:         journal, so what the budget leaves outstanding is a restart's to\n1271:         resend.  Answers the drained state.\"\n1272:   [state server now sleep!]\n1273:   (if (shutdown-enter? state server)\n1274:     (loop [state (update state :repl repl.dht/stop!)\n1275:            server (some-> server serve/stop!)\n1276:            remaining stop-ticks]\n1277:       (let [[state' server' lines _stopped?]\n1278:             (shutdown-tick state server (now))]\n1279:         (doseq [line lines]\n1280:           (println line))\n1281:         (flush)\n1282:         (cond\n1283:           (shutdown-drained? state' server') state'\n1284:           (zero? remaining) (do (println stop-timeout-text) state')\n1285:           :else (let [round-end (+ (now) tick-millis)]\n1286:                   ;; a sleep readiness ends early pumps, then sleeps the\n1287:                   ;; rest: an early wake cannot spend a round (R17)\n1288:                   (loop []\n1289:                     (sleep! (max 0 (- round-end (now))))\n1290:                     (when (< (now) round-end) (recur)))\n1291:                   (recur state' server' (dec remaining))))))\n1292:     state))\n1293: \n1294: \n1295: (defn run-owner!\n1296:   \"Serial shell owner shared by the JVM and jolt. Host policy supplies\n1297:    clock, interruptible sleep and status-aware exit. No producer evaluates.\"\n1298:   [state server headless? now sleep! exit!]\n1299:   (loop [state state\n1300:          server server\n1301:          cadence (cadence/init default-cadence)]\n1302:     (let [[state' server' lines] (step-all state server\n1303:                                            (now))]\n1304:       (doseq [line lines]\n1305:         (println line))\n1306:       (flush)\n1307:       (if (:running? state')\n1308:         (do (when (and (not headless?) (prompt-owed? state state' lines))\n1309:               (do (print prompt) (flush)))\n1310:             (let [{:keys [cadence-state sleep-ms]}\n1311:                   (cadence/cadence-step cadence\n1312:                                         (moved? state' server' lines))]\n1313:               (sleep! sleep-ms)\n1314:               (recur state' server' cadence-state)))\n1315:         (let [state' (drain-owner! state' server' now sleep!)]\n1316:           (println)\n1317:           (close-index-store! state')\n1318:           (flush)\n1319:           (exit! (exit-status state')))))))\n1320: \n1321: \n1322: #?(:cljd\n1323:    nil\n1324:    :jolt nil\n1325:    :clj\n1326:    (do\n1327:      (defn- print-prompt!\n1328:        []\n1329:        (print prompt)\n1330:        (flush))\n1331: \n1332:      (defn poll-loop!\n1333:        \"JVM policy wrapper around the portable serial owner.\"\n1334:        ([state server headless?]\n1335:         (poll-loop! state server headless? #(.halt (Runtime/getRuntime) 0)))\n1336:        ([state server headless? exit!]\n1337:         (poll-loop! state server headless? exit! (wake/make-wake)))\n1338:        ([state server headless? exit! w]\n1339:         (run-owner! state server headless? #(System/currentTimeMillis)\n1340:                     #(wake/sleep! w %)\n1341:                     (fn [status]\n1342:                       (if (zero? status) (exit!)\n1343:                           (.halt (Runtime/getRuntime) (int status)))))))\n1344: \n1345:      (defn- read-loop!\n1346:        \"The reader parks in `read-line` and appends, nudging the step owner's\n1347:         wake: a typed line ends the idle sleep at once.  It reads no state.\"\n1348:        [input w]\n1349:        (loop []\n1350:          (if-let [line (read-line)]\n1351:            (do (driver/submit-line! input line)\n1352:                (wake/nudge! w)\n1353:                (recur))\n1354:            (do (driver/submit-line! input \"(quit)\")\n1355:                (wake/nudge! w)))))\n1356: \n1357:      (defn -main\n1358:        [& args]\n1359:        (let\n1360:          [started (startup args {:persist? true})]\n1361:          (when (contains? started :exit) (exit-with! started))\n1362:          (if-some\n1363:            [refusal (:refusal started)]\n1364:            (refuse! refusal)\n1365:            (let\n1366:              [{:keys [opts state server]} started\n1367:               headless? (boolean (:headless? opts))\n1368:               w (wake/make-wake)]\n1369:              (doseq [line (banner opts)]\n1370:                (println line))\n1371:              (let\n1372:                [poller (Thread.\n1373:                          ^Runnable (fn []\n1374:                                      (poll-loop!\n1375:                                        state\n1376:                                        server\n1377:                                        headless?\n1378:                                        #(.halt\n1379:                                           (Runtime/getRuntime) 0)\n1380:                                        w)))]\n1381:                (.setDaemon poller true)\n1382:                (.start poller)\n1383:                ;; Headless attends the endpoint only: there is no reader, so the\n1384:                ;; step owner is joined until it stops, and the explicit stop\n1385:                ;; trigger\n1386:                ;; is the host signal a shutdown hook observes.  The hook appends\n1387:                ;; a\n1388:                ;; line like any producer, nudges, and then waits for the one\n1389:                ;; step\n1390:                ;; owner.\n1391:                (if headless?\n1392:                  (do (.addShutdownHook\n1393:                        (Runtime/getRuntime)\n1394:                        (Thread. ^Runnable (fn []\n1395:                                             (request-stop! state w)\n1396:                                             (.join poller ^long\n1397:                                                    stop-join-millis))))\n1398:                      (.join poller))\n1399:                  (do (print-prompt!)\n1400:                      ;; End-of-input is one way to stop; a typed `(quit)` is the\n1401:                      ;; other, and the step owner has already exited the process\n1402:                      ;; by\n1403:                      ;; the time this join is reached in that case.\n1404:                      ;; The join is unbounded: lines read before end-of-input\n1405:                      ;; still evaluate ahead of its `(quit)`, and the step\n1406:                      ;; owner bounds its own shutdown drain (`stop-ticks`)\n1407:                      ;; and exits the process itself.\n1408:                      (read-loop! (:input state) w)\n1409:                      (.join poller))))\n1410:              (.halt (Runtime/getRuntime) 0)))))))\n1411: \n1412: \n1413: #?(:jolt\n1414:    (defn -main\n1415:      \"One plain-thread owner. Stdin deposits only; EOF deposits (quit).\n1416:       One host WebSocket instance serves the shell, the head board and\n1417:       --port; every owner round polls its sockets (headless included) and\n1418:       the instance is closed before exit, however the drain ended.\n1419:       Saved flags persist in the node directory, as on the JVM (J4d):\n1420:       --reset forgets them and --no-state neither reads nor writes them.\"\n1421:      [& args]\n1422:      (let [adapter (host/websocket)\n1423:            started (startup args {:persist? true :adapter adapter})]\n1424:        (when (contains? started :exit) (exit-with! started))\n1425:        (if-some [refusal (:refusal started)]\n1426:          (refuse! refusal)\n1427:          (let [{:keys [opts state server]} started\n1428:                headless? (boolean (:headless? opts))\n1429:                eof? (atom false)\n1430:                w (wake/make-wake)\n1431:                line! (fn [line]\n1432:                        (if (nil? line)\n1433:                          (do (reset! eof? true)\n1434:                              (driver/submit-line! (:input state) \"(quit)\"))\n1435:                          (driver/submit-line! (:input state) line)))\n1436:                sleep! (fn [ms]\n1437:                         (wake/sleep-fds! w ms (:pump adapter)\n1438:                                          (when-not (or headless? @eof?) line!)))]\n1439:            (doseq [line (banner opts)] (println line))\n1440:            (when-not headless? (print prompt))\n1441:            (flush)\n1442:            ;; the exit status leaves through System/exit, which skips the\n1443:            ;; finally, so the exit path closes the instance itself too\n1444:            (try\n1445:              (run-owner! state server headless? #(System/currentTimeMillis)\n1446:                          sleep! (fn [status]\n1447:                                   ((:close-host! adapter))\n1448:                                   (wake/close! w)\n1449:                                   (flush)\n1450:                                   (System/exit status)))\n1451:              (finally\n1452:                ((:close-host! adapter))\n1453:                (wake/close! w))))))))\n1454: \n1455: \n1456: ;; =============================================================================\n1457: ;; cljs (Node): a readline handler that only appends, and one interval owner\n1458: ;; =============================================================================\n1459: \n1460: #?(:cljs\n1461:    (do\n1462:      (defn run-node!\n1463:        \"The tick owner on Node: one wake source arms exactly one timer per\n1464:         round, at the interval `cadence-step` computes over\n1465:         `default-cadence`.  Returns the wake so the composition can wire its\n1466:         line producers (the readline handlers, the stop signals) as\n1467:         `nudge!` callers.\n1468: \n1469:         `repl-step` is synchronous and the Node event loop is single\n1470:         threaded, so a tick cannot overlap itself.  The box is host cadence\n1471:         plumbing: the tick is the only reader and writer of it.  `:stopping`\n1472:         is nil while the shell runs and a tick budget afterwards: the drain\n1473:         is entered when there is an endpoint to stop, a head board that\n1474:         still owes its stop-and-drain, or custody owes a control write\n1475:         (`shutdown-enter?` -- a REPL without --port drains its owed custody\n1476:         writes too), the head board and the endpoint are asked to stop\n1477:         once, and the drain steps at the base interval until the endpoint\n1478:         has stopped AND the board owes nothing AND custody owes nothing\n1479:         (`shutdown-drained?`) or the budget ends.  `exit!`, the process\n1480:         exit by default, is the seam a test injects to drive the owner to\n1481:         its end.\"\n1482:        ([state server rl] (run-node! state server rl #(js/process.exit %)))\n1483:        ([state server rl exit!]\n1484:         (let [box (atom {:state state :server server :stopping nil\n1485:                          :cadence (cadence/init default-cadence)})\n1486:               wake-ref (volatile! nil)\n1487:               finish! (fn []\n1488:                         (wake/disarm! @wake-ref)\n1489:                         (when rl (.close rl))\n1490:                         (close-index-store! (:state @box))\n1491:                         (exit! (exit-status (:state @box))))\n1492:               tick (fn []\n1493:                      ;; The interval timer this namespace replaced fired\n1494:                      ;; again whatever happened, so a tick whose body throws\n1495:                      ;; must not kill the owner: arm the fallback first and\n1496:                      ;; let the body's own `arm!` replace it. `finish!`\n1497:                      ;; disarms, so a finished owner parks nothing.\n1498:                      (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)\n1499:                      (let [{:keys [state server stopping cadence]} @box]\n1500:                        (if (nil? stopping)\n1501:                          (let [[state' server' lines] (step-all state server\n1502:                                                                 (js/Date.now))]\n1503:                            (reset! box {:state state'\n1504:                                         :server server'\n1505:                                         :stopping nil\n1506:                                         :cadence cadence})\n1507:                            (doseq [line lines]\n1508:                              (js/console.log line))\n1509:                            (cond\n1510:                              (:running? state')\n1511:                              (let [{:keys [cadence-state sleep-ms]}\n1512:                                    (cadence/cadence-step\n1513:                                      cadence (moved? state' server' lines))]\n1514:                                (swap! box assoc :cadence cadence-state)\n1515:                                (when (and rl (prompt-owed? state state' lines))\n1516:                                  (.prompt rl))\n1517:                                (wake/arm! @wake-ref sleep-ms))\n1518: \n1519:                              (shutdown-enter? state' server')\n1520:                              (do (swap! box assoc\n1521:                                         :state (update state' :repl repl.dht/stop!)\n1522:                                         :server (when server'\n1523:                                                   (serve/stop! server'))\n1524:                                         :stopping stop-ticks)\n1525:                                  (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis))\n1526: \n1527:                              :else (finish!)))\n1528:                          (let [[state' server' lines _stopped?]\n1529:                                (shutdown-tick state server (js/Date.now))]\n1530:                            (swap! box assoc :state state' :server server')\n1531:                            (doseq [line lines]\n1532:                              (js/console.log line))\n1533:                            (cond\n1534:                              (shutdown-drained? state' server') (finish!)\n1535:                              (zero? stopping)\n1536:                              (do (js/console.log stop-timeout-text) (finish!))\n1537:                              :else (do (swap! box assoc :stopping (dec stopping))\n1538:                                        (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)))))))]\n1539:           (vreset! wake-ref (wake/make-wake tick))\n1540:           (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)\n1541:           @wake-ref)))\n1542: \n1543:      (defn -main\n1544:        [& args]\n1545:        (let [started (startup args {:persist? true})]\n1546:          (when (contains? started :exit) (exit-with! started))\n1547:          (if-some [refusal (:refusal started)]\n1548:            (refuse! refusal)\n1549:            (let [{:keys [opts state server]} started\n1550:                  readline (when-not (:headless? opts) (js/require \"readline\"))\n1551:                  rl (when readline\n1552:                       (.createInterface readline\n1553:                                         #js {:input (.-stdin js/process)\n1554:                                              :output (.-stdout js/process)\n1555:                                              :prompt prompt}))]\n1556:              (doseq [line (banner opts)]\n1557:                (js/console.log line))\n1558:              ;; The tick owner arms its first round, and the composition wires\n1559:              ;; the\n1560:              ;; deposit it hands each line producer with the nudge: a typed line\n1561:              ;; ends the idle sleep at once.\n1562:              (let [w (run-node! state server rl)]\n1563:                (if rl\n1564:                  (do (.on rl \"line\" (fn [line]\n1565:                                       (driver/submit-line! (:input state) line)\n1566:                                       (wake/nudge! w)))\n1567:                      (.on rl \"close\" (fn []\n1568:                                        (driver/submit-line! (:input state)\n1569:                                                             \"(quit)\")\n1570:                                        (wake/nudge! w)))\n1571:                      (.prompt rl))\n1572:                  ;; Headless has no reader, so the explicit stop trigger is the\n1573:                  ;; host\n1574:                  ;; signal: it appends a line, nudges, and returns, like any\n1575:                  ;; producer.\n1576:                  (doseq [signal [\"SIGINT\" \"SIGTERM\"]]\n1577:                    (.on js/process signal (fn []\n1578:                                             (request-stop! state\n1579:                                                            w))))))))))))\n1580: \n1581: \n1582: ;; =============================================================================\n1583: ;; cljd: a stdin listener that only appends, and one periodic timer owner\n1584: ;; =============================================================================\n1585: \n1586: #?(:cljd\n1587:    (do\n1588:      (defn- write-line!\n1589:        [message]\n1590:        (println message))\n1591: \n1592:      (defn- print-prompt!\n1593:        []\n1594:        (.write io/stdout prompt)\n1595:        (.flush io/stdout))\n1596: \n1597:      (defn run-dart!\n1598:        \"One wake source owns the tick: exactly one `Timer` armed per round, at\n1599:         the interval `cadence-step` computes over `default-cadence`, because a\n1600:         synchronous poll loop would deadlock the Dart event loop: IO never\n1601:         progresses, so `blocked` never clears.  Returns the wake so the\n1602:         composition can wire its line producers (the stdin listener, the\n1603:         stop signals) as `nudge!` callers.\n1604: \n1605:         `:stopping` is nil while the shell runs and a tick budget afterwards:\n1606:         the drain is entered when there is an endpoint to stop, a head\n1607:         board that still owes its stop-and-drain, or custody owes a\n1608:         control write (`shutdown-enter?` -- a REPL without --port drains\n1609:         its owed custody writes too), the head board and the endpoint are\n1610:         asked to stop once, and the drain steps at the base interval\n1611:         until the endpoint has stopped AND the board owes nothing AND\n1612:         custody owes nothing (`shutdown-drained?`) or the budget ends, so\n1613:         the host does not exit with a live listener or a dropped\n1614:         release.  `exit!`, the process exit by default, is the seam\n1615:         a test injects to drive the owner to its end.\"\n1616:        ([state server headless?]\n1617:         (run-dart! state server headless? #(io/exit %)))\n1618:        ([state server headless? exit!]\n1619:         (let [box (atom {:state state :server server :stopping nil\n1620:                          :cadence (cadence/init default-cadence)})\n1621:               wake-ref (volatile! nil)\n1622:               finish! (fn []\n1623:                         (wake/disarm! @wake-ref)\n1624:                         (close-index-store! (:state @box))\n1625:                         (exit! (exit-status (:state @box)))\n1626:                         nil)\n1627:               tick (fn []\n1628:                      ;; The periodic timer this namespace replaced fired\n1629:                      ;; again whatever happened, so a tick whose body throws\n1630:                      ;; must not kill the owner: arm the fallback first and\n1631:                      ;; let the body's own `arm!` replace it. `finish!`\n1632:                      ;; disarms, so a finished owner parks nothing.\n1633:                      (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)\n1634:                      (let [{:keys [state server stopping cadence]} @box\n1635:                            now (.-millisecondsSinceEpoch\n1636:                                  (dart-core/DateTime.now))]\n1637:                        (if (nil? stopping)\n1638:                          (let [[state' server' lines]\n1639:                                (step-all state server now)]\n1640:                            (reset! box {:state state'\n1641:                                         :server server'\n1642:                                         :stopping nil\n1643:                                         :cadence cadence})\n1644:                            (doseq [line lines]\n1645:                              (write-line! line))\n1646:                            (cond\n1647:                              (:running? state')\n1648:                              (let [{:keys [cadence-state sleep-ms]}\n1649:                                    (cadence/cadence-step\n1650:                                      cadence (moved? state' server' lines))]\n1651:                                (swap! box assoc :cadence cadence-state)\n1652:                                (when (and (not headless?)\n1653:                                           (prompt-owed? state state' lines))\n1654:                                  (print-prompt!))\n1655:                                (wake/arm! @wake-ref sleep-ms))\n1656: \n1657:                              (shutdown-enter? state' server')\n1658:                              (do (swap! box assoc\n1659:                                         :state (update state' :repl repl.dht/stop!)\n1660:                                         :server (when server'\n1661:                                                   (serve/stop! server'))\n1662:                                         :stopping stop-ticks)\n1663:                                  (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)\n1664:                                  nil)\n1665: \n1666:                              :else (finish!)))\n1667:                          (let [[state' server' lines _stopped?]\n1668:                                (shutdown-tick state server now)]\n1669:                            (swap! box assoc :state state' :server server')\n1670:                            (doseq [line lines]\n1671:                              (write-line! line))\n1672:                            (cond\n1673:                              (shutdown-drained? state' server') (finish!)\n1674:                              (zero? stopping) (do (write-line! stop-timeout-text)\n1675:                                                   (finish!))\n1676:                              :else (do (swap! box assoc :stopping (dec stopping))\n1677:                                        (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)\n1678:                                        nil))))))]\n1679:           (vreset! wake-ref (wake/make-wake tick))\n1680:           (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)\n1681:           @wake-ref)))\n1682: \n1683:      (defn -main\n1684:        [& args]\n1685:        (let [started (startup args {:persist? true})]\n1686:          (when (contains? started :exit) (exit-with! started))\n1687:          (if-some [refusal (:refusal started)]\n1688:            (refuse! refusal)\n1689:            (let [{:keys [opts state server]} started\n1690:                  headless? (boolean (:headless? opts))]\n1691:              (doseq [line (banner opts)]\n1692:                (write-line! line))\n1693:              ;; The tick owner arms its first round, and the composition wires\n1694:              ;; the\n1695:              ;; deposit it hands each line producer with the nudge: a typed line\n1696:              ;; ends the idle sleep at once.  A timer fires only on the event\n1697:              ;; loop, so arming before the producers are wired races nothing.\n1698:              (let [w (run-dart! state server headless?)]\n1699:                (if headless?\n1700:                  ;; Headless has no reader, so the explicit stop trigger is the\n1701:                  ;; host signal: it appends a line, nudges, and returns, like\n1702:                  ;; any\n1703:                  ;; producer.\n1704:                  (-> (.watch io/ProcessSignal.sigint)\n1705:                      (.listen (fn [_signal] (request-stop! state w))))\n1706:                  (do (-> io/stdin\n1707:                          (.transform (.-decoder convert/utf8))\n1708:                          (.transform (convert/LineSplitter.))\n1709:                          (.listen (fn [line]\n1710:                                     (driver/submit-line! (:input state) line)\n1711:                                     (wake/nudge! w))\n1712:                                   .onDone (fn []\n1713:                                             (driver/submit-line! (:input state)\n1714:                                                                  \"(quit)\")\n1715:                                             (wake/nudge! w))))\n1716:                      (print-prompt!))))))))\n1717: \n1718:      (defn ^{:dart/name main} run-main\n1719:        [args]\n1720:        (apply -main args))))\n1721: docs/agents/build-n-test.md\n1: ---\n2: description: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n3: ---\n4: \n5: # BUILD & TEST\n6: \n7: ## Commands\n8: \n9: ```sh\n10: # Tests (Babashka runner)\n11: bb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\n12: bb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\n13: bb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n14: # One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\n15: bb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\n16: bb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\n17: bb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\n18: bb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\n19: bb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n20: bb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n21: bb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\n22: bb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n23: bb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n24: # Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n25: # Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n26: # (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n27: # Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n28: # under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n29: # On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n30: # seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n31: # Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n32: # Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n33: # not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n34: # which lanes may overlap.\n35: #\n36: # When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n37: # Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n38: # default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n39: # 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n40: # wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n41: # test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n42: # 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n43: # Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n44: # yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n45: # and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n46: # (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n47: # While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n48: # or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n49: # When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\n50: # (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\n51: # never the whole suite (owner ruling 2026-11-01; see Gate Ladder G0).\n52: # At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n53: # G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n54: # Full repo-wide `bb test` runs twice daily on the remote hosts (03:00/17:00 VN);\n55: # a MERGE needs only the affected tests (bb test:changed) -- owner ruling 2026-11-01.\n56: # Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n57: # for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n58: # contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n59: # overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n60: # Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n61: # 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n62: # compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n63: # not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\n64: npm test             # Node.js tests\n65: \n66: # Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n67: # `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n68: # without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n69: # Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\n70: bb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\n71: bb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n72:                          # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n73:                          # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n74:                          # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n75: ```\n76: \n77: ### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n78: \n79: `bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\n80: reach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\n81: merge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\n82: tests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\n83: A walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\n84: fs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\n85: io/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n86: (let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\n87: lanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\n88: needed builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n89: `:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\n90: typo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\n91: land a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\n92: change (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\n93: antlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\n94: as for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\n95: dao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n96: (`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\n97: and spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n98: \n99: ### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\n100: \n101: ```sh\n102: bb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\n103: bb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\n104: bb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\n105: bb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\n106: bb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\n107: bb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\n108: bb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\n109: bb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\n110: bb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\n111: ```\n112: \n113: `test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\n114: the JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\n115: `--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\n116: `affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\n117: the full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\n118: semantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\n119: \n120: Shard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\n121: `affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\n122: `test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\n123: in an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\n124: therefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\n125: `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\n126: Prerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\n127: serially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\n128: (`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\n129: \n130: Each invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\n131: `selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\n132: and `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\n133: namespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\n134: Any uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\n135: The final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\n136: JVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\n137: failure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\n138: Dry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\n139: selection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\n140: The final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\n141: record is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\n142: `:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\n143: `:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\n144: \n145: `test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\n146: set, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\n147: \n148: +----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n149: | Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\n150: +==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\n151: | Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\n152: | configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\n153: +----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n154: | Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\n155: |                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\n156: +----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n157: | Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\n158: | `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\n159: +----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n160: | Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\n161: |                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\n162: +----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n163: | Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\n164: +----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n165: \n166: Mixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\n167: set, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\n168: the base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\n169: `bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\n170: its zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\n171: staged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\n172: `git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\n173: under `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\n174: executable examples or validate documentation links.\n175: \n176: Routing prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\n177: means the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\n178: is satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\n179: and failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\n180: quick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\n181: `:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\n182: results, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\n183: \n184: **Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\n185: tri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\n186: `bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\n187: `bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\n188: serialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\n189: demo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\n190: lanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\n191: available. **Never run the slow tier, `bb test:all`, or `nightly.clj` on the\n192: laptop** — the slow tier is 52 long namespaces whose JVM processes each\n193: multi-thread (test workers, JIT, GC threads sized by core count); four of them\n194: GC-thrashing at once drove a laptop load average over 100 on 2026-11-01 while\n195: making no progress (the remote hosts run this tier: jupiter 5,645 s at 12\n196: workers, shiva comparable). Do not run another lane set concurrently on the\n197: laptop either.\n198: \n199: #### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\n200: \n201: `bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\n202: recorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\n203: `deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\n204: It also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\n205: classes and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\n206: removed or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\n207: digest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\n208: invalidate the complete-build stamp.\n209: \n210: `bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\n211: `package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\n212: environment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\n213: reuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\n214: ensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\n215: \n216: Stamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\n217: lock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\n218: that escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\n219: not a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\n220: `node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\n221: still require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\n222: does not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\n223: builds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\n224: \n225: Laptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\n226: the unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\n227: both completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\n228: Sharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\n229: outer command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\n230: in **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\n231: rounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\n232: timed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\n233: a median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\n234: \n235: ### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n236: \n237: `bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\n238: one or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\n239: is by longest namespace prefix matching.\n240: \n241: ```sh\n242: bb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\n243: bb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\n244: bb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\n245: bb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\n246: bb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\n247: bb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\n248: bb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n249: ```\n250: \n251: #### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n252: \n253: - **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n254: - **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n255: - **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n256: - **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n257: - **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n258: - **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n259: \n260: Prerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\n261: and lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\n262: selected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n263: \n264: #### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n265: \n266: 1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n267:    **Failure-reproduction rule (owner ruling 2026-11-01): when a test fails anywhere — a\n268:    gate, the nightly, or a delegate report — reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the\n269:    failing test, never the whole suite.** JVM: `clojure -M:test -n <ns>` (a single\n270:    deftest: add `-v <ns>/<var-name>`). Node: `node target/node-tests.js --test=<ns>`\n271:    (built by `bb build:yin-repl-node` first only if that test needs the Node REPL).\n272:    Dart: `bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only <ns>`. Fix against the failing test alone,\n273:    re-run it green, then climb back up (G1 for the home subsystem, G2 for dependents).\n274:    Never reproduce or chase a failure with the full suite or the slow tier — the full\n275:    suite is not a debugging tool, and running it locally for one failing test is the\n276:    exact misuse that drove a laptop load average over 100 (2026-11-01). A failure that\n277:    only reproduces under the full suite's concurrency is diagnosed with the suite's own\n278:    logs (its per-shard/per-test output), not by re-running the suite repeatedly.\n279: 2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n280: 3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n281: 4. **G3 Master (merged in place by the 2026-11-01 owner ruling)**: The landing gate\n282:    for a change is the **tests that cover the feature being built** — `bb test:changed`\n283:    (G2) already includes the reverse-require closure of the changed files, and a wide\n284:    change still routes to the full quick lane. The complete suite is no longer a\n285:    per-merge gate: it runs twice a day on the remote hosts (see \"Cadence\" below), so a\n286:    break is caught within hours without charging every merge ~400 minutes of slow tier.\n287: 5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge\n288:    (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n289:    (owner decision 2026-10-12), and implicitly twice daily by the scheduled run below.\n290:    G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n291: \n292: \n293: The linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\n294: slice L5) is two tests:\n295: \n296: - `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n297:   processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n298:   publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n299:   name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n300:   and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n301:   every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n302:   `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n303:   `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n304: - `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n305:   scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n306:   signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n307:   exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n308:   REPL.\n309: \n310: ```sh\n311: # ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\n312: clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\n313: clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\n314: clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n315: \n316: # ClojureDart\n317: clj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n318: \n319: # Linting\n320: clj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n321: ```\n322: \n323: The Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n324: [--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\n325: namespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\n326: Dart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\n327: with its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n328: `--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\n329: exits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\n330: each of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\n331: script compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n332: <namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n333: (greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n334: `flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\n335: under `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\n336: different namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\n337: package:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n338: \n339: `flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\n340: compile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\n341: aggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\n342: stale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\n343: before relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n344: \n345: ## Test tiers: quick and slow\n346: \n347: The suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n348: (owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n349: \n350: - **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n351:   `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n352:   guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n353: - **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n354:   `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n355: - **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n356:   takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n357:   deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n358:   **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n359:   wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n360:   `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n361:   in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n362:   with the method below before changing the cut.\n363: - **Cadence (owner ruling 2026-11-01):** a merge needs only the tests that cover the\n364:   feature being built — the affected/dependent set from `bb test:changed` (or\n365:   `bb test:sub <own> --slow` when the change touches slow-tagged code). The FULL suite,\n366:   including the slow tier, runs **twice a day: 03:00 and 17:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh** on the\n367:   remote hosts (`src/dev/nightly.clj`; 03:00 catches the day's work before the owner\n368:   stops coding, 17:00 gives a result they can act on before sleeping). The measured long\n369:   set is ~400 minutes of serial slow-tier work, so it cannot be a per-merge cost; a\n370:   break is instead caught by the next scheduled run, at most ~12 hours later.\n371:   The schedule is expressed as an hourly cron plus a Vietnam-time guard inside the\n372:   driver, so it is immune to the DST shifts of either host's local timezone.\n373: \n374: Measured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\n375: servers\" for how to run them):\n376: \n377: | Run | Time |\n378: |---|---|\n379: | Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n380: | Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n381: | **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n382: | **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n383: \n384: The quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\n385: Some quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n386: `prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\n387: under the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\n388: A lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n389: \n390: **How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\n391: run it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\n392: otherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n393: \n394: ```sh\n395: NS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n396: # timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n397: #   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n398: # and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n399: # load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n400: ```\n401: \n402: **Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\n403: shards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\n404: tests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\n405: JVM test namespace under `test/`:\n406: \n407: ```sh\n408: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n409: # Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\n410: set -u\n411: export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\n412: cd \"$1\" || exit 2\n413: PAR=\"${2:-8}\"\n414: mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\n415: eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n416: OUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\n417: ts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\n418: echo \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n419: npm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n420: bb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n421: bb gen:antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n422: SUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n423: : > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n424: for s in $SUBS; do\n425:   bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n426:   cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n427:   echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\n428: done\n429: # coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\n430: find test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\n431: sort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\n432: comm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\n433: echo \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n434: run_shard() {\n435:   s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n436:   args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n437:   t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n438:   echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n439: }\n440: export -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\n441: printf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\n442: echo \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n443: ```\n444: \n445: **The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n446: \n447: ```sh\n448: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n449: # Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\n450: set -u\n451: export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\n452: cd \"$1\" || exit 2\n453: PAR=\"${2:-6}\"\n454: mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\n455: eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n456: OUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\n457: run_ns() {\n458:   n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n459:   clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n460:   echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n461: }\n462: export -f run_ns; export OUT\n463: echo \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n464: xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\n465: echo \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n466: ```\n467: \n468: ## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n469: \n470: Two Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\n471: reached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\n472: and `git`.\n473: \n474: | Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\n475: |---|---|---|\n476: | `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\n477: | `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\n478: \n479: **Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\n480: that is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\n481: owner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n482: \n483: ### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n484: \n485: Rsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\n486: would take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\n487: directories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\n488: branch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\n489: the host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n490: \n491: ```sh\n492: # once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\n493: ssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n494:   URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n495:   git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n496:   cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n497:   git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n498: ```\n499: \n500: To test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n501: `git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\n502: was never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n503: \n504: ### A clone is only current if it updated from real master\n505: \n506: A scratch clone's `origin/master` ref is only as fresh as the place it fetches\n507: from — and a clone made from another scratch clone inherits that clone's *stale*\n508: refs. Measured on shiva 2026-11-02: the `dg` clone was created from the `p4`\n509: scratch clone, whose local `master` ref had not moved since October 26 because\n510: every lane on it ran detached off fetched branches; the `dg` lane's\n511: `checkout origin/master` therefore produced a commit 436 commits behind real\n512: master, and the G3 Dart leg honestly reported failures (the datagram fix) for a\n513: tree that did not contain them.\n514: \n515: Before any lane runs in a clone — every time, not just the first:\n516: \n517: ```sh\n518: cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\n519: git remote get-url origin          # must be the GitHub URL, never another scratch clone\n520: git fetch -q origin \"+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\"\n521: git checkout -q --detach origin/master   # or the lane's branch\n522: git merge-base --is-ancestor <the-commit-hash-you-pushed> HEAD && echo CURRENT\n523: ```\n524: \n525: If `origin` is not GitHub, repoint it (`git remote set-url origin \"$URL\"`). If\n526: the `merge-base` check fails, the clone's bookkeeping lies: refetch, or delete\n527: and recreate the clone from GitHub directly. Never trust a hash the clone\n528: reports without comparing it to the hash your push from the laptop printed.\n529: \n530: ### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n531: \n532: A non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\n533: tools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\n534: into the clone:\n535: \n536: ```sh\n537: export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\n538: cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\n539: mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\n540: mise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\n541: eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\n542: mise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\n543: ```\n544: \n545: Activating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\n546: (flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\n547: find flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n548: \n549: ### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n550: \n551: \"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\n552: CPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n553: `test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\n554: avoidable\"). The laptop additionally never runs the slow tier or the nightly\n555: driver (see the sharded-lane section) — a full slow tier there reached a load\n556: average over 100 on 2026-11-01; the remote hosts own that tier. On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\n557: lanes may overlap, with these limits:\n558: \n559: - **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n560:   (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n561:   runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n562:   `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n563:   the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n564:   gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n565:   by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n566:   verified by a measured run.\n567: - **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n568:   JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n569: - **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n570:   `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n571: - **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n572:   run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n573: - Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n574: \n575: The first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\n576: this rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\n577: separate Dart clone.\n578: \n579: ### Scratch-clone first-run gaps (two measured sightings)\n580: \n581: A fresh scratch clone needs two things the lane runner does not yet set up\n582: itself; until the runner heuristic is fixed, do them by hand before the\n583: first lane:\n584: \n585: 1. **`npm install`** — the Node tests require npm deps (`@noble/hashes` was\n586:    the measured miss on shiva 2026-11-02: the cljs lane ran but the\n587:    node-tests bundle died with `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`).\n588: 2. **`bb gen:antlr`** — the `prerequisites` heuristic in\n589:    `src/dev/affected.clj` decides `:gen:antlr` from the JVM lane's reach\n590:    only (\"ANTLR classes are JVM only\" — stale: python keeps multi-target\n591:    parsers), so a cljs/cljd-only selection that reaches\n592:    `yang.python.antlr.parser` (e.g. `bb test:sub:cljs yang.base` via\n593:    `yin.repl.frontends`) skips the generation and the shadow build dies on\n594:    the missing `Python3Lexer.js`. Sighted independently on the p4 lane\n595:    (PhpLexer ClassNotFoundException) and the yang.base cljs lane\n596:    (2026-11-02). A fix round is in flight on `fix/gen-antlr-prereq-heuristic`.\n597: \n598: ### Ledger commit hygiene: never commit a conflict marker\n599: \n600: The append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`,\n601: `orchestrator-logs/*.md`, `docs/agents/routing-status.md`) resolve rebase\n602: and merge conflicts by the union rule, which means conflicts are common —\n603: and a conflict marker that reaches a commit silently corrupts the record\n604: (measured: the 2026-10-30 rebase landed a log with three committed\n605: `<<<<<<<`/`>>>>>>>` markers and dropped records the union was supposed to\n606: keep; caught by the architect four rounds later). Before committing ANY\n607: change to a ledger file:\n608: \n609: ```sh\n610: git diff --cached | grep -E '^\\+(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' && echo CONFLICT-MARKERS-STAGED\n611: git grep -E '^(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' -- docs/agents/known-gaps.md orchestrator-logs docs/agents/routing-status.md\n612: ```\n613: \n614: If either hits, the commit stops until the union is redone. When resolving\n615: a ledger conflict, prefer regenerating the union from both sides' section\n616: lists over hand-merging, and re-read the result against BOTH parents before\n617: staging.\n618: \n619: ### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\n620: \n621: The jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\n622: [--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\n623: process (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\n624: `build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\n625: only thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\n626: (strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\n627: 5-6-hour run.\n628: \n629: On the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\n630: \n631: - jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\n632:   only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\n633:   sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\n634: - the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\n635:   (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\n636:   `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\n637:   `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\n638:   the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\n639: - **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\n640:   (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\n641:   The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\n642:   baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\n643:   attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\n644:   passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\n645:   timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\n646:   merge-decision qualification runs.\n647: \n648: Suites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\n649: `step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\n650: under the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\n651: entry, not to re-run under the same bound.\n652: \n653: ### The full run\n654: \n655: Run long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\n656: serial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\n657: the Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\n658: tasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\n659: lane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\n660: same commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n661: \n662: ```sh\n663: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n664: # ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\n665: set -u\n666: export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\n667: DART=\"${2:-}\"\n668: cd \"$1\" || exit 2\n669: mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\n670: eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n671: LOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\n672: ts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\n673: echo \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n674: npm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n675: bb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n676: bb gen:antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n677: bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n678: lane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n679:          echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\n680: lane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\n681: lane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\n682: if [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n683:   ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n684:     bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n685:     lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\n686: fi\n687: wait\n688: echo \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n689: ```\n690: \n691: ```sh\n692: ssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n693: ```\n694: \n695: Watch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\n696: press Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\n697: and `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\n698: write to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n699: `bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\n700: scanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n701: (measured 2026-10-11).\n702: \n703: ### Known differences from the laptop\n704: \n705: - **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` on Linux —\n706:   MEASURED AND FIXED (2026-11-02).** The old entry inferred that \"a second UDP bind on\n707:   a taken port behaves differently on Linux\"; confirmed, with the cause. Dart's\n708:   `RawDatagramSocket.bind` defaults to `reuseAddress: true`, and **Linux honours\n709:   `SO_REUSEADDR` for UDP**, so a second bind to a port a reuse-enabled socket already\n710:   holds *succeeds* (`strace`: `SO_REUSEADDR=1` on both sockets, no `SO_REUSEPORT`, both\n711:   kernel `bind()` calls succeed); macOS refuses with errno 48. The seam was correct\n712:   throughout — given a genuinely exclusive holder (`reuseAddress: false`) it deposits\n713:   `bind-failed` in ~10 ms with errno 98. The test asserted one host's binding policy\n714:   rather than the contract, so it now binds its own exclusive blocker\n715:   (`RawDatagramSocket.bind \"127.0.0.1\" 0 .reuseAddress false .reusePort false`) and\n716:   points the seam at that port: the failure path is exercised on both hosts. Verified\n717:   on shiva: the namespace passes 5/5.\n718: - A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n719:   printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n720: - Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n721:   under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n722: \n723: ### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\n724: \n725: Owner, verbatim: \"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\n726: completely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\n727: branch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\n728: changes\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \"it should not be for\n729: only a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\"\n730: \n731: Two tiers, by target and purpose:\n732: \n733: - **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-full.sh` on\n734:   shiva, tmux session `nightly`, scheduled 03:00 daily through the\n735:   orchestrator's automation; it fetches and checks out origin/master\n736:   before anything runs). Every lane, every host target: the JVM full\n737:   suite, the full Node suite, the full Dart suite. Its results are a\n738:   post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\n739:   finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\n740: - **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\n741:   changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\n742:   touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\n743:   when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\n744:   a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\n745:   re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\n746:   to master is what puts it under the nightly.\n747: \n748: An orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\n749: sign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\n750: nightly owns the follow-up.\n751: \n752: **The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling, restored 2026-10-31 —\n753: a dedup pass dropped this section along with a stale duplicate of the\n754: two-tier policy; both stand):** before any merge into master, the\n755: `dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is what everything is\n756: built on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the subsystem's tests,\n757: `--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded surfaces) is part of every\n758: branch gate regardless of which subsystems the diff's own changes\n759: select, because a change can break the foundation without touching it.\n760: A red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or diagnosed as\n761: pre-existing by the owning seat.\n762: \n763: ### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n764: \n765: Shiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\n766: it, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\n767: Node REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\n768: not recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\n769: lanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\n770: extra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n771: \n772: ### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\n773: \n774: First full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\n775: ANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\n776: replaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\n777: (`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\n778: **Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\n779: laptop — route Node lanes to the laptop or shiva); **Dart quick tier\n780: rc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\n781: (`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\n782: taken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\n783: is the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\n784: time matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\n785: medians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\n786: \n787: One exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\n788: `gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\n789: same time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\n790: \"Patch does not apply\" or \"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\".\n791: Run them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\n792: The fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\n793: generator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\n794: one week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\n795: \n796: ### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\n797: \n798: Owner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\n799: are installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\n800: orchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \"run tests remotely\n801: over SSH\" to \"delegate whole work agents remotely\": a heavy implementation or\n802: verification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\n803: suites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\n804: orchestrator's laptop unloaded.\n805: \n806: The pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\n807: reference):\n808: \n809: 1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\n810:    prepare the scratch clone per \"Get the code there\".\n811: 2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\n812:    the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \"$(mise env -s\n813:    bash)\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\n814:    A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\n815:    session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\n816:    inside the same command.\n817: 3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\n818:    write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\n819:    (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\n820:    on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\n821: 4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\n822:    carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\n823:    logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\n824: 5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\n825:    one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\n826:    from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\n827: 6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\n828:    Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\n829:    review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\n830:    Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\n831:    apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\n832: \n833: Known state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\n834: jolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\n835: owner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\n836: the full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\n837: its 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\n838: host is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\n839: \n840: ## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n841: \n842: Write tests before implementing features (TDD).\n843: \n844: Tests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n845: \n846: When implementing a feature or fix:\n847: 1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n848: 2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n849: 3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n850: \n851: This ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n852: \n853: ## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n854: \n855: A single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n856: \n857: 1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n858:    ```sh\n859:    clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n860:    ```\n861: \n862: 2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n863:    Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n864: \n865: 3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n866:    Common patterns:\n867:    - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n868:    - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n869:    - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n870: \n871: 4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n872: .cljstyle\n1: ;; :namespaces is disabled project-wide. It rewrites ns forms, which is\n2: ;; unsafe on this multi-platform codebase in two distinct ways:\n3: ;; 1. ClojureDart uses string-prefixed :import forms (e.g.\n4: ;;    (:import [\"dart:core\" BigInt DateTime])); the rule rewrites these\n5: ;;    into JVM-style forms, which the cljd compiler then fails to resolve.\n6: ;; 2. .cljc files that guard a JVM-only :import/:require clause with\n7: ;;    #?(:cljd nil :clj ...) rely on :cljd appearing FIRST (the cljd\n8: ;;    host-eval pass also matches :clj, so first-match-wins order is\n9: ;;    load-bearing — see the reader-conditional trap in project memory).\n10: ;;    The rule reorders reader-conditional branches alphabetically\n11: ;;    (:clj before :cljd), silently re-breaking the guard on every\n12: ;;    `cljstyle fix` — this bit dao.jing.dht.node.cljc and cost a lost\n13: ;;    commit before being traced back here.\n14: {:files {:extensions #{\"blog\" \"chp\" \"clj\" \"cljc\" \"cljd\" \"cljs\" \"edn\" \"jolt\"},\n15:          :ignore #{\".cpcache\" \".git\" \".shadow-cljs\" \"node_modules\" \"target\"}},\n16:  :rules {:blank-lines {:enabled? true, :max-consecutive 2},\n17:          :eof-newline {:enabled? true},\n18:          :indentation {:enabled? true},\n19:          :namespaces {:enabled? false},\n20:          :whitespace\n21:            {:enabled? true, :insert-missing? true, :remove-trailing? true}}}\n22: "}],"_meta":{"codex/nodeReplExecutionDurationMs":16},"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"node_repl","tool":"js","arguments":{"code":"var harness = await fs.readFile(root + '/src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj', 'utf8'); var main = await fs.readFile(root + '/src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc', 'utf8'); var show = (p,s,a,b) => nodeRepl.write(p+'\\n'+s.split('\\n').slice(a-1,b).map((l,i)=>`${i+a}: ${l}`).join('\\n')); show('harness', harness, 410, 525); nodeRepl.write(main.split('\\n').flatMap((l,i)=>/sleep-fds!|defn.*step|owner|host-pump/.test(l)?[`${i+1}: ${l}`]:[]).join('\\n')); var entries = await fs.readdir(root,{recursive:true}); nodeRepl.write(entries.filter(p=>/(^|\\/)(AGENTS\\.md|BRIEF-j6g[^/]*|\\.cljstyle)$/.test(p) && !/node_modules|\\.git\\//.test(p)));","title":"Locate owner rounds and existing guard/report paths"},"result":null,"error":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
